        "type": "text",
        "text": "You are a judge of syntactic circuits. Work only in the current folder.\n1. Read RULES.md completely, including every settled case at its end.\n2. Read the whole file meetings-07-advanced.p01.csv: columns n, text, sop, alt, label_state. Each row is a message with a circuit (sop) written by one formalizer and a different circuit (alt) written by another; either may be empty (no circuit). Both saw only the words of the message.\n3. Judge every row's sop by RULES.md and write meetings-07-advanced.p01.verdicts.jsonl in this folder, one JSON line per row of the CSV: {\"n\": <number>, \"grade\": \"L8\" or \"L1\"..\"L6\", \"note\": \"<one sentence>\"}. L8 accepts sop. For a rejection (also when sop is empty) the note must quote the words of the message in single quotes and name the wrong statement or the lost information, and the line must also carry \"sop\": a corrected circuit, written in the syntax schema of RULES.md, valid for the message text. You may take alt as the corrected circuit when it is right; otherwise write your own. Use real newlines escaped as \\n inside the JSON string.\nWork in batch: read the whole CSV, then write the whole verdicts file at once (you may write it in a few large parts); never edit line by line. Judge fairly: a plausible or incomplete reading that the next layer can continue from is acceptable; reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information. Do not edit or create any other file."
      }
    ],
