    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "The output truncation is a problem. I need a more compact display. Let me instead print rows to files that I read with read_file with offsets, or print more compactly.\n\nBetter approach: write dumps to scratchpad files, chunked (e.g., 10 rows per file), and read them one at a time. But that's many files. Alternatively print with bash in smaller chunks (e.g., 8 rows per call) — each row ~1.5KB, 8 rows = 12KB, under 16K. But then 150/8 = 19 calls. That's fine but many turns.\n\nActually output limit is 16000 chars per tool response. So chunks of ~10 rows would work (avg 1.1KB/row → 11KB). Let's do 10 rows per call → 15 calls. Each call yields both sop and alt; I need mostly sop but alt helps as correction candidate.\n\nAlternatively I could reduce output by only printing sop when it differs little... no, I need both.\n\nHmm, but each call is a separate message in my context containing ~11KB ≈ 3000 tokens. 15 calls = 45k tokens. That's acceptable.\n\nActually, maybe better: use read_file on a dump file with offsets — same cost. Let's just do bash dumps in chunks of ~12 rows and be economical: print text, sop; and alt only if it differs? I need alt only when rejecting. But I want to decide rejection from sop; alt can be fetched later for the rejected rows in a batch. That saves a lot of context!\n\nPlan: \n- Pass 1: for each row, print TEXT and SOP only (compact). ~700 bytes/row → 20 rows per call → 8 calls. \n- Pass 2: for rejected rows, print alt.\n\nBut careful: grading fairly may need comparison with alt when sop is subtly wrong. I think judging sop alone is fine per rules (\"Do not compare a circuit with the one you would have written\").\n\nAlso need to verify: sop may be empty? All 150 have non-empty sop.\n\nLet me also note that rule \"A program has already checked that every word of the message stands in exactly one value.\" Hmm, but the examples show e.g. row 8 sop: text words: \"my volunteering shifts keep getting mixed up with work stuff, so please add a calendar called Volunteering just for them\". In sop, \"so\" as conjunction, \"just\" modifier of c4, \"for\" marker... \"them\" head n5. Wait \"called Volunteering just for them\" — c4 nonfinite verb \"called\", complement n4 (Volunteering), modifier \"just\"; relation r2 marker \"for\" from n4 to n5. Hmm — \"just for them\" modifies \"add a calendar ... just for them\". Starting at n4 (Volunteering) - it ties to the name. Acceptable-ish? The \"for\" relation start at n4 (a nominal of the clause) — the rule says relation start: the clause, or any nominal of that clause grammar allows. Is n4 a nominal of clause c4? Yes, c4 nonfinite. Hmm, but \"for\" relation attaches \"just for them\" to the calendar phrase. Starting at n4 passes I think (marker \"for\", end n5). Fine.\n\nRow 8 sop: c1 \"keep\" declarative subject \"my volunteering shifts\"; c2 declarative auxiliary \"getting\" verb \"mixed\" modifier \"up\". Question: \"keep getting mixed up\" — \"keep\" verb, then \"getting mixed up with work stuff\" nonfinite. Is c2 \"declarative\" with auxiliary getting, verb mixed — hmm. \"getting\" is the main verb of that nonfinite clause? Or an auxiliary? Written as declarative, auxiliary \"getting\", verb \"mixed\". Is that a wrong statement? c2 is a nonfinite clause \"getting mixed up with work stuff\" — the form should be nonfinite, not declarative. Rule L1: \"A clause that the circuit writes is wrong. Its form is wrong: a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse; a clause with a verb written as fragment.\"\n\nHmm — \"a clause with a verb written as `fragment`\" is L1. Is \"declarative\" vs \"nonfinite\" for \"getting mixed up\" an L1 error? Form list: declarative, interrogative, imperative, nonfinite, fragment. L1 explicitly lists: clear question as imperative/declarative or reverse; clause with verb as fragment. The reverse of \"clear question as declarative\" — a declarative written as interrogative. Nonfinite written as declarative: not explicitly listed. Hmm.\n\nIn row 8, sop c2 declarative auxiliary=\"getting\" verb=\"mixed\". Alternatively alt treats \"getting\" as nonfinite verb and \"mixed up\" as separate nonfinite clause with relation to \"stuff\". Which is right? \"keep getting mixed up with work stuff\" — \"getting mixed up\" is passive progressive: \"getting\" auxiliary, \"mixed\" participle. Treating \"getting\" as auxiliary and \"mixed\" as verb is defensible. Marking the clause as declarative is arguably wrong (it's nonfinite: \"getting mixed up\"). But is that a rejection? The words: \"my volunteering shifts keep getting mixed up with work stuff\". There's one clause with subject \"shifts\" and verb \"keep\"; \"getting mixed up with work stuff\" is its complement — a nonfinite clause. Writing it as `declarative` form: is that a wrong statement? It says the clause makes a declarative statement. But it has no subject... Hmm.\n\nI need to be pragmatic and consistent: judge primarily on clear violations listed in grades. For form issues, the rules stress: \"form is wrong: a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse; a clause with a verb written as fragment\". Nonfinite vs declarative isn't listed explicitly, but \"the split into clauses, and the form of each clause\" must be right. A nonfinite clause (a verb form that cannot make a sentence by itself) written as declarative is a form error → L1 arguably. But this is very common in these formalizers perhaps.\n\nGiven 150 rows and this ambiguity, my policy: \n- If a subordinate/participial clause with no subject is marked declarative but the verb/words are correctly grouped and tied, I'd accept (L8) — but hmm. Risk of being too lenient or too harsh. The rules' L1 emphasis is on question/command confusion, verb-as-fragment, wrong verb, misplaced aux/negation, words held in another clause, missing ellipsis.\n\nI'll accept form \"declarative\" for nonfinite-looking clause only if... hmm. Let me think about it more carefully with the \"next layer\" test: the next layer reads \"c2 declarative auxiliary getting verb mixed\". Can it continue? It knows subject is \"shifts\" via c1. The mislabeling of form as declarative doesn't create a wrong statement that must be undone — it's a minor form nuance; the relation r3 from c1 to c2 shows it's subordinate. I lean accept.\n\nBut wait, in row 8 there's a bigger issue: \"so\" coordination k1 conjunct=$c1 conjunct=$c3. \"my shifts keep getting mixed up ..., so please add a calendar\" — \"so\" joins c1 (declarative) with c3 (imperative). Is that allowed? The settled case says: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3\" — but that's specifically about a coordination joining an imperative clause with a clause that ends a relation with a marker (i.e., the clause stands inside another). Here \"so\" joins main clause c1 with command c3. That's ordinary: \"my shifts get mixed up, so please add a calendar\". Fine.\n\nAlso r1: marker \"with\" from c2 to n2 (\"work stuff\") — \"mixed up with work stuff\". Fine.\n\n\"just\" as modifier of c4: acceptable (modifier for non-nominal non-marker words).\n\n\"for\" relation from n4 to n5 — starting at \"Volunteering\" (n4) which is the complement of c4. Hmm, \"just for them\" — the rule about relation start: \"the clause, or any nominal of that clause that grammar allows\". n4 is a nominal of c4 (\"called Volunteering\"). Grammar allows \"for them\" to attach to... semantically to \"add a calendar for them\". Starting at n4 vs c3 or c4. Is starting at n4 (the name \"Volunteering\") ruled out by the words? It's a nominal of the same clause chain. I'd accept — permissive reading. Hmm, but one could argue \"just for them\" belongs to the \"add\" clause. The rule says the start of a relation with a marker: any nominal of that clause that grammar allows. n4 is in clause c4 which is tied to c3... The relation's start being a nominal of another clause is L3 (\"a start the words rule out, such as a nominal of another clause\"). Ugh. n4 is a nominal of c4, so starting r2 at n4 while semantically it attaches to the \"add\" clause. But the \"rule out\" language suggests only starts that are clearly impossible. \"a calendar called Volunteering just for them\" — \"for them\" most naturally attaches to \"add\". But attaching to \"Volunteering\" is not clearly ruled out by grammar? Actually \"just for them\" right after \"Volunteering\" — grammatically it could be \"a calendar called Volunteering, just for them\" (i.e., the calendar is just for them). That's a plausible reading. Accept.\n\nOK. Given volume, I should set consistent principles and just go through. Let me be moderately lenient: accept unless a specific L-rule clearly applies.\n\nNow let me get the data efficiently. I'll print text+sop only, 20 rows per call, and note the row index ordering by n.\n\nLet me be careful about output size. I'll compute per-chunk sizes. Let me write a python script that prints rows text+sop for a range and skips alt.\n\nActually, maybe print in chunks sized by total characters (~14000 limit). Let me do that automatically: chunk rows into groups whose printed size < 13000 chars.\n\nLet me first do rows 0-19 text+sop.\n\nAlso I should track verdicts in a scratchpad as I go, so that after summarization I can recover. I'll write notes per chunk into scratchpad.\n\nLet's go. Use bash with python3 printing to stdout with a size cap per call.\n\nI'll structure the call: python3 print rows i..j. Given avg row printed size with sop only ~600-700 chars, 20 rows ≈ 13KB. Ok.\n\nLet me start with rows 0-19 (already partially seen, but reprint text+sop for clarity).",
