    "text": "Good, 8850 chars for 16 rows. So ~550 chars/row. I can do ~25 rows per call (13750). Let me judge these 16 first.\n\nRow 0 (n=8): discussed. sop: c2 declarative auxiliary getting verb mixed. Hmm. Also n4 \"Volunteering\" complement of c4, r2 from n4 to n5 \"for them\". I'll accept? Let me consider \"just\" as modifier of c4 — the word \"just\" grades \"for\"; rule: \"A word that grades a linking word ... may be a modifier of the clause the relation belongs to\". c4 holds the start of r2 (n4 in c4). OK consistent. \n\nIs form declarative for c2 a \"wrong statement\"? The clause \"getting mixed up with work stuff\" is nonfinite (no subject). Calling it declarative... In RULES \"form is nonfinite (a verb form that cannot make a sentence by itself: 'facing left', 'delete it' after 'to')\". \"getting mixed up with work stuff\" cannot make a sentence by itself. So the form should be nonfinite; declarative is wrong. But is it in the L1 list? The L1 text: \"Its form is wrong: a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse; a clause with a verb written as fragment.\" It gives examples with colon — arguably illustrative, but the \"or the reverse\" and \"clause with a verb as fragment\" are the listed types. Hmm. \"a clear question written as declarative, or the reverse\" — the reverse of \"question as declarative\" is \"declarative as interrogative\". So the L1 form examples concern questions/fragments.\n\nI think for consistency I'll treat nonfinite-vs-declarative on a subjectless subordinate clause as acceptable (the next layer can continue; subject comes from the main clause). This keeps me from rejecting large numbers of rows on a debatable point. But I should check whether it's frequent and whether there are clearer markers. Actually, wait — is it truly acceptable? Consider \"my shifts keep getting mixed up with work stuff\": the SOP says c2 declarative aux=getting verb=mixed. A next layer would read \"getting\" as auxiliary and \"mixed\" as the verb — that's the passive participle read, which is right-ish. Fine, L8.\n\nRow 1 (n=9): \"ok so before we plan the trip, make me a Travel calendar and then put a Packing reminder on it Friday\".\nsop: c1 imperative discourse=ok modifier=so verb=make comp=n3(me),n4(a Travel calendar); r1 marker=before from c1 to c2; c2 declarative verb=plan subject=we complement=the trip; k1 conjunction and conjunct=c1 conjunct=c3; c3 imperative modifier=then verb=put complement=n5 (a Packing reminder) complement=n7 (Friday); r2 marker=on from c3 to n6 (it).\n\nIssue: \"ok so\" — \"so\" here: rules say \"And\", \"Then\" or \"So\" at the start of a message with nothing before it to join is a discourse word. Here \"so\" is at start (after \"ok\") → discourse word allowed; held as modifier — rule says \"'Now' at the start ... may be a discourse word or a modifier\". For \"so\", the settled case says discourse word. But is modifier wrong? Hmm. It's not a linking word here (nothing to join before). Holding \"so\" as a modifier — the rules' settled case says at the start with nothing before it, \"So\" is a discourse word. Does holding it as modifier lose info? It grades it as a manner word. Minor. Also note: is \"so\" a modifier of c1 allowed? Hmm, \"so\" is not a modifier semantically. But L2 is about content words in discourse. Reverse (discourse word as modifier) isn't listed. I'd accept.\n\nBigger: \"before we plan the trip\" — the temporal subordinate clause placed before main clause. r1 marker=before from c1 (make) to c2 (plan). That's fine: \"before\" links main clause and the sub clause. Start at c1 — the words: \"before we plan the trip\" precedes \"make me...\". The relation start: hmm, \"the clause, or any nominal of that clause\". Starting at c1 = the imperative \"make\" clause. Since \"before\" stands inside the imperative clause at its beginning, starting at c1 is fine.\n\nc3: complement n5 and n7 \"Friday\". \"put a Packing reminder on it Friday\" — Friday is a time. Rules: \"A day, an hour or a length of time set off by commas after a phrase with a marker ... belongs to the clause: a relation from the clause with no marker passes\". Here no commas, but holding \"Friday\" as a complement of the put clause is plausible (a second thing acted on? no — \"Friday\" is time). Hmm, complement holds \"the nominals the verb takes, whatever they are to it\" — Friday as complement of \"put\" — the rules say complement is judged only in terms of which clause's complement (L3: \"A nominal is the complement of the wrong clause\"). Friday is in the right clause. Also which nominal is a complement vs modifier isn't heavily judged (\"What a complement is to the verb ... is not judged\"). Accept.\n\nWait, \"on it Friday\" — hmm ok.\n\nRow 2 (n=18): \"new calendar named Study, colour it grey\"\nsop: n1 nominal \"new calendar\"; c1 nonfinite verb=named complement=n2 (Study); c2 imperative verb=colour complement=n3 (it) complement=n4 (grey); c3 fragment complement=n1; k1 coordination conjunct=c3 conjunct=c2; r1 from n1 to c1.\n\nIssue: c2 \"colour it grey\" — \"grey\" as a complement of the clause. Per settled case: \"make the box red\": \"red\" is a modifier of the clause, since \"the box\" is a complement. So \"colour it grey\": \"grey\" should be a modifier, not a complement. Is holding \"grey\" as a complement a rejection? Rules: complement holds \"the nominals the verb takes, whatever they are to it\". The case says for \"the box red\", \"red\" is a modifier — the reason given: \"since 'the box' is a complement\". So after the complement, a word describing how the thing should be is a modifier. Also the settled case: \"A word after a nominal that is a complement of its clause and says how the thing should be is a modifier of the clause\" — \"held as a dependent of the nominal, the word becomes part of the description ... L3\". Here it's held as a complement, not a dependent. Hmm — the failure specified is only for dependent.\n\nAlso \"gold\" in row 62 alt: \"colour it gold\" → c1 imperative verb=colour modifier=gold complement=n1(it). And sop for row 62: fragment with colour/it/gold all complements — that's clearly L1/L5: \"colour it gold\" is a command with verb \"colour\"; held as fragment → L1 per settled case (\"A command needs its verb... held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1\").\n\nFor row 2, \"grey\" as complement of the imperative clause: the rule text says complement holds the nominals the verb takes \"whatever they are to it\" — this is lenient. And \"What a complement is to the verb ... is not judged\". But is a nominative after the object a complement wrong? The case says red is a modifier \"since 'the box' is a complement\". Strictly this suggests grey should be a modifier, but the failure grade listed for that case is about dependent. Since the rule explicitly says \"What is not judged: What a complement is to the verb\", and here grey is a nominal in the right clause, I'd accept. Actually the case \"make the box red\" says \"red\" is a modifier of the clause — meaning if you write it as complement, is it wrong? The not-judged section covers what the complement is *to the verb* (recipient, etc.), not whether it is a complement at all. Hmm, \"grey\" as a complement of \"colour\" — \"colour it grey\" — colour takes \"it\" as object; \"grey\" is a result predicate. Calling grey a complement says the verb takes grey as an argument, which is roughly true for \"colour\" (colour X Y). L5? no. I'll accept — plausible reading, next layer can continue.\n\nAlso c3 fragment complement=n1 tied by k1 to c2. \"new calendar named Study, colour it grey\" — first part is a fragment naming a thing, then imperative. Coordination joining a fragment with an imperative clause: is that a wrong conjunct? L3 rule about coordination with wrong conjunct applies to \"a clause between two conjuncts\". Two side-by-side units — comma join. Acceptable.\n\nAlso c1 nonfinite \"named\" tied to n1 via r1. Good.\n\nRow 3 (n=20): \"since Family is already blue, can you create a Kids calendar in yellow so I can tell them apart?\"\nsop: r1 marker=since from c2 to c1; c1 declarative verb=is modifier=already modifier=blue subject=n1(Family); c2 interrogative aux=can verb=create subject=you complement=n3; r2 marker=in from n3 to n4(yellow); r3 marker=so from c2 to c3; c3 declarative aux=can verb=tell modifier=apart subject=I complement=them.\n\nGood. \"blue\" as modifier of the clause — settled case: modifier. Fine. Accept L8.\n\nRow 4 (n=22): \"I keep missing bin days, so let's have a Chores calendar, bright magenta so it stands out\"\nsop: c1 declarative verb=keep subject=I; c2 nonfinite verb=missing complement=n2; k1 so conjunct c1 c3; c3 imperative verb=let complement=n3(\"'s\"); c4 nonfinite verb=have complement=n4(a Chores calendar); n5 nominal \"bright magenta\" (bright dependent, magenta head); r1 marker=so from c4 to n5; n6 it; c5 declarative verb=stands modifier=out subject=it; r2 c1→c2; r3 c3→c4; r4 from n4 to n5.\n\nIssues: \"bright magenta so it stands out\" — sop ties n5 to c4 (have a Chores calendar) with marker \"so\" and then c5 (\"it stands out\") is tied to...? r1 goes from c4 to n5. c5 \"so it stands out\" — the \"so\" is used as the marker of r1 (from c4 to n5), so c5 is not joined to anything! \"so it stands out\" — the subordinating \"so\" belongs to c5. So c5 is untied → L6? L6: \"Something is left untied. A clause or a nominal is tied to nothing inside its sentence.\"\n\nc5 = clause \"it stands out\" with subject \"it\". Is it tied? Nothing references c5. So L6.\n\nAlso r1: \"bright magenta so it stands out\" — the \"so\" as marker between c4 and n5 is wrong-ish; the marker \"so\" should tie c4 (or n4) to c5. Hmm — r1 from c4 to n5 with marker \"so\": that says \"bright magenta\" relates to \"have\" via \"so\". The word \"so\" stands before c5 (\"so it stands out\"). A relation's ends should be the clauses/nominals connected by the marker. \"so\" connects \"have a Chores calendar, bright magenta\" with \"it stands out\" → from c4 to c5. So r1 has the wrong end (n5 instead of c5) → L3 (\"A relation has the wrong end\"). And c5 untied → L6. The first applicable grade: L3 before L6. So L3.\n\nHmm, but wait: also n5 \"bright magenta\" (a nominal) as subject-ish...? It's complement-less; n5 is the end of r1. And \"it\" refers to the calendar — fine.\n\nActually is L3 right? The relation r1 marker \"so\" from c4 to n5: end is n5 which is the colour phrase. The \"so\" in the message introduces the clause \"it stands out\". So the wrong end. L3. Note must quote words: 'so it stands out'. And correction: r1 from c4 to c5 (or from n4 to c5), c5 tied.\n\nLet me write correction:\n@c1 declarative verb=keep subject=$n1\n@c2 nonfinite verb=missing complement=$n2\n@n2 nominal dependent=bin head=days\n@k1 coordination conjunction=so conjunct=$c1 conjunct=$c3\n@c3 imperative verb=let complement=$n3\n@n3 nominal head=\"'s\"\n@c4 nonfinite verb=have complement=$n4\n@n4 nominal dependent=a dependent=Chores head=calendar\n@n5 nominal dependent=bright head=magenta\n@r1 relation from=$n4 to=$n5  (no marker) — or \"bright magenta\" as complement of c4? Simpler: relation from c4 to n5 without marker.\n@n6 nominal head=it\n@c5 declarative verb=stands modifier=out subject=$n6\n@r2 relation marker=so from=$c4 to=$c5\n@r3 relation from=$c1 to=$c2\n@r4 relation from=$c3 to=$c4\n\nThat covers all words. Good.\n\nRow 5 (n=26): \"new calendar in sky blue please\" — fragment: n1 \"new calendar\", r1 marker in from n1 to n2 (\"sky blue\": dependent sky head blue), c1 fragment discourse=please complement=n1. Good — L8.\n\nRow 6 (n=27): \"make a calendar for Podcast, something warm-coloured\"\nsop: c1 imperative verb=make complement=n1(a calendar); r1 marker=for from n1 to n2(Podcast); n3 nominal head=something dependent=warm dependent=coloured; r2 no marker from n1 to n3.\n\nHmm: \"something warm-coloured\" — is \"something\" the head with \"warm-coloured\" as dependent? Tokenization: text has \"warm-coloured\" as one token presumably; sop splits into \"warm\" and \"coloured\" as two dependents. Hmm, that suggests the tokens are \"warm\" and \"coloured\"? The text says \"warm-coloured\". Maybe the tokenizer splits on hyphen. Then \"something warm coloured\": head=something, dependents warm, coloured — acceptable. Or \"coloured\" head with warm dependent. Either fine.\n\nr2: ties n3 to n1 without marker: \"a calendar ... something warm-coloured\" (apposition). Acceptable. L8.\n\nRow 7 (n=28): \"add a Reading calendar, any colour that isn't used yet\"\nsop: c1 imperative verb=add complement=k1 (coordination of n1 and n2); r1 marker=that from n2 to c2; c2 declarative aux=is negation=n't verb=used modifier=yet.\n\nHmm: negation \"n't\" as a word — tokenizer splits \"isn't\" into \"is\" and \"n't\". aux=\"is\", verb=\"used\". \"any colour that isn't used yet\" — passive: \"is\" auxiliary, \"used\" verb. OK. But c2 form declarative with no subject: relative clause \"that isn't used yet\" — subject is \"that\". Held: aux is, negation n't, verb used. Form declarative — relative clause, acceptable-ish.\n\nc1 complement = k1 (coordination): \"add a Reading calendar, any colour that isn't used yet\" — \"any colour...\" is appositive complement. Fine. L8.\n\nRow 8 (n=29): \"one more calendar, mint colour\" — fragment: n1 \"one more calendar\", n2 \"mint colour\" (mint dependent, colour head), c1 fragment complement=n1, r1 n1→n2 no marker. Fine, L8.\n\nRow 9 (n=33): \"call the Family calendar Home from now on\"\nsop: c1 imperative verb=call modifier=on complement=n1(the Family calendar) complement=n2(Home); r1 marker=from from c1 to n3(now).\n\nHmm: \"from now on\" — settled case: \"A fixed phrase of time or manner ('from now on', 'after that', 'for now', ...) may be held whole as a modifier.\" Here it's split: r1 marker=from from c1 to n3 \"now\", and \"on\" as modifier of c1. That's a plausible reading too? The settled case says the phrase \"from now on\" may be held whole as a modifier — it permits that, but is splitting wrong? \"from\" as marker, \"now\" as a nominal, \"on\" as a modifier. The words are all in the right clause. Is there a wrong statement? \"Home\" as complement of call — good (\"call X Y\" — Home is the name). L8 I think. The words aren't lost; the reading \"relation marked from, starting at the clause\" is allowed.\n\nRow 10 (n=38): \"if nobody minds, rename Family to Household and make it green\"\nsop: r1 marker=if from c2 to c1; c1 declarative verb=minds subject=nobody; c2 imperative verb=rename complement=n2(Family); r2 marker=to from c2 to n3(Household); k1 and conjunct=c2 c3; c3 imperative verb=make modifier=green complement=n4(it).\n\nGood. \"green\" modifier — matches settled case. L8.\n\nRow 11 (n=39): \"the kids keep asking which calendar is theirs - could you rename Kids stuff to School and clubs?\"\nsop: n1 \"the kids\"; c1 form=interrogative verb=keep subject=n1 — WRONG: \"the kids keep asking...\" is declarative, not interrogative! L1 (a declarative written as interrogative — \"the reverse\").\n\nWait, \"the kids keep asking which calendar is theirs\" — declarative. sop writes c1 interrogative. That's L1: \"Its form is wrong: a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\". So L1.\n\nAlso c3 \"which calendar is theirs\" interrogative — that's a clause opening with a question word inside another clause → should be declarative per settled case (\"A clause that opens with a question word inside another clause ... states: its form is declarative ... Written as interrogative it is L1\"). So two L1 errors? Actually \"the kids keep asking which calendar is theirs\" — the embedded question. Per settled case, its form is declarative. So c3 interrogative = L1 too.\n\nGrade L1. Quote: 'the kids keep asking which calendar is theirs'. Correction needed: full circuit.\n\nCorrection for row 11:\n@n1 nominal dependent=the head=kids\n@c1 declarative verb=keep subject=$n1\n@c2 nonfinite verb=asking\n@r1 relation from=$c1 to=$c2\n@n2 nominal dependent=which head=calendar\n@c3 declarative verb=is subject=$n2 complement=$n3\n@n3 nominal head=theirs\n@r2 relation from=$c2 to=$c3\n@c4 interrogative auxiliary=could verb=rename subject=$n4 complement=$n5\n@n4 nominal head=you\n@n5 nominal dependent=Kids head=stuff\n@r3 relation marker=to from=$c4 to=$k1\n@n6 nominal head=School\n@n7 nominal head=clubs\n@k1 coordination conjunction=and conjunct=$n6 conjunct=$n7\n\nAll words: the kids keep asking which calendar is theirs could you rename Kids stuff to School and clubs. Yes: \"the\" in n1, \"kids\" n1, \"keep\" c1, \"asking\" c2, \"which\" n2, \"calendar\" n2, \"is\" c3, \"theirs\" n3, \"could\" c4, \"you\" n4, \"rename\" c4, \"Kids\" n5, \"stuff\" n5, \"to\" marker, \"School\" n6, \"and\" k1, \"clubs\" n7, \"?\" punctuation. Good.\n\nRow 12 (n=40): \"hmm, the name Work is confusing now that I freelance too; change it to Agency work, thanks\"\nsop: c1 imperative discourse=hmm verb=change discourse=thanks complement=n4(it); n1 \"the name\"; n2 \"Work\"; c2 declarative verb=is modifier=confusing modifier=now subject=n1; r1 marker=that from c2 to c3; n3 I; c3 declarative verb=freelance modifier=too subject=I; n4 it; r2 marker=to from c1 to n5(Agency work); k1 coordination conjunct=c1 conjunct=c2; r3 from n1 to n2.\n\nIssues: \"the name Work is confusing\" — n2 \"Work\" tied to n1 by r3 (apposition) — fine. \"now that I freelance too\" — r1 marker \"that\" from c2 to c3: hmm, \"now that\" — the marker is \"that\" and \"now\" is a modifier of c2. Plausible: settled case allows fixed phrases whole as modifier; here \"now\" modifier of clause, \"that\" marker of relation to c3. Acceptable.\n\nk1: coordination joins c1 (imperative \"change\") with c2 (declarative \"the name Work is confusing\") by \";\" with no conjunction. Hmm — the semicolon joins two sentences: \"hmm, the name Work is confusing now that I freelance too; change it to Agency work, thanks\". Is a coordination of a declarative statement and an imperative command wrong? The L3 rule: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3\" — that's about the clause standing inside another. Here c1 doesn't stand inside c2, and there's no marker relation. Joining two independent clauses (statement + command) with a coordination — the schema's coordination puts wires side by side. A command can't stand inside another clause but side-by-side with a separate sentence is fine (the settled case about \"And\" at the start of a later sentence allows a coordination of the clauses of the two sentences). Though that case was about adjacent sentences. Acceptable.\n\nL8? The \"thanks\" as discourse of c1 — allowed (discourse word may be in any clause). \"hmm\" discourse - fine. So L8? One more check: c2 complement... fine. Accept L8.\n\nRow 13 (n=47): \"rename that one Hobbies\" — c1 imperative verb=rename complement=n1 (nominal head=Hobbies dependent=that dependent=one).\n\nHmm: \"that one\" with head \"Hobbies\": merged \"that one\" and \"Hobbies\" into one nominal where \"Hobbies\" is head. Is that a rejection? Two nominals merged → L3 (\"two nominals are merged. The head is wrong.\"). Well, \"rename that one Hobbies\": complements = \"that one\" (object) and \"Hobbies\" (new name). The circuit merges them into a single nominal with head \"Hobbies\" — the object \"that one\" is lost as a separate thing, and \"Hobbies\" is presented as the thing described by \"that one\", which is a wrong statement: \"that one Hobbies\" as one nominal describing a thing. Compare settled case: \"the node Intro\" may be a dependent in the noun's nominal or a separate nominal tied by a relation — both pass. That's for a name right after a noun. Here \"that one Hobbies\" is not such a pattern: \"that one\" is a pronoun object and \"Hobbies\" is a name given to it, the pattern \"rename X Y\" — like \"call the Family calendar Home\" (row 9) where the sop kept them as separate complements. Here merging them into one nominal means the words form one nominal with head \"Hobbies\", dependents \"that\" and \"one\" — that makes \"Hobbies\" a thing described as \"that one\". That's a wrong statement about which words describe which thing → L3.\n\nHmm, is that too harsh? The pattern \"rename that one Hobbies\" is like \"rename X to Y\". Merging loses the fact that two things stand (the object and the new name). I think L3 is defensible: \"two nominals are merged\". The message gives two nominals: \"that one\" and \"Hobbies\". Actually, does it? \"Hobbies\" could be read as an appositive name in the same nominal (\"that one Hobbies\"). Hmm. But \"rename\" takes two complements. I lean reject L3? Let me think about the \"next layer\" test: next layer reads n1 = nominal(head Hobbies, dep that, dep one). It would then think the thing to rename is called \"Hobbies\"... which is the new name, so renaming \"that one\" (unspecified) to Hobbies is lost — the target of rename is lost. Yes: the object of \"rename\" is lost as a distinct nominal. I'll grade L3.\n\nCorrection:\n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"rename\" complement=$n1 complement=$n2\n@n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"that\" head=\"one\"\n@n2 syntax.nominal head=\"Hobbies\"\n\nRow 14 (n=51): \"I'd like Work shown in purple\"\nsop: n1 I; c1 declarative aux=\"'d\" verb=like subject=I; n2 Work; c2 nonfinite verb=shown; r1 marker=in from c2 to n3(purple); c3 fragment complement=n2; r2 from n2 to c2.\n\nGood: \"I'd like Work shown in purple\" — \"Work\" as object of like? Here it's held as a fragment c3 complement=n2, tied to c2 (shown). Hmm: \"like Work shown\" — \"Work\" should be the complement of c1 (like) and the subject of \"shown\". Written as a separate fragment clause tied to c2. Is that acceptable? The words of \"Work\" are held in a fragment clause rather than in c1's complement. Per L1: \"Words of one written clause are held in another\" — hmm, \"Words of one written clause are held in another\" means words belonging to a clause are put in a different clause. Is there a written clause where Work belongs? The message: \"I'd like Work shown in purple\" — the matrix clause \"I'd like [Work shown in purple]\" where \"Work shown in purple\" is a nonfinite clause with subject \"Work\". Writing \"Work\" as its own fragment tied to c2 (shown) is a podd. It's tied, so not L6. The subject isn't written, but writing a subject is optional (\"The subject, when the words show one\" must be right — failing to write a subject isn't an error unless... L4 is writing a subject the words don't show).\n\nI'd accept L8. Alternatively the fragment c3 with complement n2 and r2 from n2 to c2 — the nominal is tied. Fine.\n\nRow 15 (n=57): \"ok so paint the Gym calendar black and move yoga onto it\"\nsop: c1 imperative discourse=ok verb=paint modifier=black complement=n1; k1 coordination conjunction=so conjunction=and conjunct=c1 c2; n1 the Gym calendar; c2 imperative verb=move complement=n2(yoga); r1 marker=onto from c2 to n3(it).\n\nIssue: \"so\" held as a conjunction of the same coordination as \"and\" — the coordination has two conjunction values (\"so\" and \"and\"). \"so\" is at the start after \"ok\": per settled case it's a discourse word (nothing before to join). Holding it as a conjunction of the coordination between c1 and c2 says \"so\" joins paint and move — wrong statement (a coordination has one conjunction; and \"so\" as a conjunction here claims a causal link between the two). Hmm, is that a rejection? The rule for \"So\" at start: \"with nothing before it to join, is a discourse word\". So writing it as a conjunction joins c1 and c2 — a statement the words don't support (it joins nothing). Is this covered? L3 \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\"? Not exactly. L4: \"The circuit states what the message does not say: ... a relation or a coordination between wires that the message does not relate.\" Hmm — \"so\" joining paint...move? The message: \"ok so paint ... and move yoga onto it\". \"so\" at the start with nothing before it — it relates nothing. Written as a conjunction of the coordination between the two commands, it states a link. I lean L4? Hmm.\n\nBut also grammatically the coordination itself (paint ... and move) is fine; the issue is the extra conjunction value \"so\". Two conjunction values on one coordination — the schema has \"conjunction\". Having both is at minimum a malformed/duplicate value. Does it state something wrong? It asserts \"so\" joins c1 and c2. Per the settled case, \"so\" at start with nothing before it is a discourse word, so writing it as a conjunction is wrong. Which grade? I'd say L3 (\"Words stand in the wrong wire\") or L4. Hmm.\n\nHmm, let me consider: this might be common in these packets. Let me look for how often \"so\"/\"and\"/\"then\" at start is held as conjunction. In row 1: \"ok so before we plan...\" — \"so\" modifier. Fine.\n\nFor row 15: honestly the risk is the \"so\" as a second conjunction. A next layer reading k1 with conjunction \"so\" and \"and\" between two imperatives — would it need to undo? It'd interpret \"so\" as joining paint→move, i.e., a causal relation: \"paint ... so move yoga\" which is wrong-ish; the \"so\" modifies the whole request as an opener (\"ok so\"). Also \"ok\" is discourse — fine.\n\nI'll grade L3 with note quoting 'ok so paint'. Hmm, wait. Alternatively L4. The rules' L3 first bullet: \"Words stand in the wrong wire\" — \"so\" stands in the conjunction slot of a coordination where it links nothing. I'll use L3.\n\nCorrection: c1 imperative discourse=ok discourse=so verb=paint modifier=black complement=n1; k1 coordination conjunction=and conjunct=c1 c2; ...\n\nLet me now record chunk 1 verdicts in scratchpad and continue.\n\nActually, let me hold off writing scratchpad until I have all data; but the risk of summarization losing my judgments is real. I'll write incremental scratchpad notes per chunk. Better: accumulate verdicts as JSON lines in a scratchpad file after each chunk. Since verdicts must be written at the end in bulk, store in scratchpad and then assemble. Actually I could also append to the verdicts file in parts (the task allows writing in a few large parts). But simpler: keep scratchpad records with full JSON lines including sop corrections (escaped newlines). Let me do that as I go: an array of the JSON lines.\n\nCareful: sop strings contain double quotes; the JSON line needs escaping. I'll build the JSONL via a script at the end from a scratchpad file containing per-row verdict + corrected sop in a plain format? Simpler: write scratchpad file with entries like:\n\nn=8|L8|note text\n\nand for rejections include sop in a block with real newlines. Then use python (bash) to build the JSONL? But bash writing to file — the task says don't create other files; scratchpad is separate and allowed. But then reading them back and assembling: I can write a python script that parses my scratchpad note file and produces JSONL. That's doable but fiddly. Alternatively I write the final JSONL myself via write_file in a few parts, with \\n escapes — I must be careful manually escaping quotes. That's error-prone with 150 rows of sop.\n\nBetter plan: store the corrected sops in a scratchpad as a simple format and let python do JSON escaping.\n\nSimplest robust approach: I keep a scratchpad file \"verdicts.txt\" where each rejection is recorded as:\n\n# n=22 L3 <note>\n<sop lines...>\n\nand L8 rows as \"# n=8 L8\". Then a python script converts: for each block, if grade L8 → {\"n\":n,\"grade\":\"L8\",\"note\":\"...\"} else include sop with \\n joined.\n\nThat works, and I write the scratchpad incrementally after each chunk of ~25 rows. Then at the end produce the verdicts file with a python script that does json.dumps. But wait: is using python to write the output file acceptable? Yes — the requirement is only about not editing line by line and not creating other files. The python writes only the verdicts file.\n\nHowever, scratchpad writes are per-call with full content (write replaces). Each chunk append means rewriting the whole accumulating file — that gets long (150 rows of sops). It'd double context. Alternative: use scratchpad only for a compact record (n + grade + note), and keep the sop corrections... I need them somewhere. Hmm.\n\nAlternative approach: build the verdicts file incrementally via bash appending blocks, then a final conversion. But \"Do not edit or create any other file\" — creating a temp file in /tmp is outside the current folder; the instruction says work only in the current folder and don't create other files. Safer: use scratchpad dir (that's the sanctioned private area).\n\nPlan: after each chunk of rows, use `unified_harness_scratchpad` write to a per-chunk file (e.g. \"chunk01.txt\") with the rejection sops and all grades compactly. That keeps each write small. At the end, read all chunk files back (I'd have to read them — extra context) or better: at the end, run a python script that reads the scratchpad directory files and generates the JSONL. But does the sandboxed env let python read the scratchpad dir path? The scratchpad is a real directory; I can find its path. Hmm, `unified_harness_scratchpad` is a tool; its directory path unknown but likely under ~/.vibe/... I could find it via filesystem search. Risky.\n\nAlternative: avoid the intermediate entirely. Write the final verdicts JSONL in parts using write_file (part 1: rows 1-50, etc.) — but write_file replaces whole content; so I'd need to accumulate. I could instead write part files... not allowed (only the verdicts file).\n\nHmm. Option: use bash heredoc to append JSON lines to the verdicts file, one part per chunk (5-6 appends). That's \"writing in a few large parts\" as explicitly allowed. The risk is escaping double quotes inside JSON strings manually. I can avoid that: in the heredoc, write the circuit with single-line sops but manually escaping quotes: RULES schema uses double-quoted values. In JSON I need \\\" escapes. In a bash heredoc with 'EOF' quoted, backslashes are literal, so I can write \\\" directly and it stays as \\\" in the file — which is exactly what JSON needs. \n\nAnd newlines: I need \"\\n\" inside the JSON string — literal backslash-n, which in a quoted heredoc stays literal. \n\nSo: append with `cat >> file <<'EOF'` ... lines ... EOF. Each JSON line written exactly as needed. 150 lines with sops: manageable but long. My context cost: I generate the content once (~1.5× the sops I saw). That's maybe 25k tokens of output total. Acceptable-ish but large. Total output ~50KB across the file. Hmm, that's a lot of generation but necessary.\n\nWait, but I must be careful: if a later chunk needs re-writing (mistake), I can regenerate the whole file. Prefer to write appends only after careful judgment.\n\nAnother concern: /tmp temp file for the python check. I'll write a python check reading the folder only — I can do the JSON validation inline with python3 -c reading file. Fine, no extra file.\n\nLet me structure: 6 parts of ~25 rows each. Actually rows vary in size; let me read ~20-25 rows per pass, judge, then append those verdicts immediately (so context holds only one chunk at a time plus the append). That keeps things clean.\n\nChunk 1 done (rows 0-15). Let me write the first append now for rows 0-15, then continue reading.\n\nVerdicts for chunk 1 (rows index 0..15, n=8,9,18,20,22,26,27,28,29,33,38,39,40,47,51,57):\n\nn=8: L8\nn=9: L8\nn=18: L8\nn=20: L8\nn=22: L3 (so relation wrong end + c5 untied)\nn=26: L8\nn=27: L8\nn=28: L8\nn=29: L8\nn=33: L8\nn=38: L8\nn=39: L1\nn=40: L8\nn=47: L3\nn=51: L8\nn=57: L3\n\nHmm, for L8 rows the note — rules example shows note \"\" for L8. The task says note: \"<one sentence>\". For L8, I'll write a brief sentence. The rules example has \"\" for L8. Task says one sentence per line; I'll write a short positive sentence. That's fine (check.mjs may just check format; there is no check.mjs here anyway).\n\nHmm, wait: the task says the note is one sentence; for L8 a short statement like \"Accepted: ...\" is fine.\n\nNow let me write the append for these 16 rows. Note: must use real newlines escaped as \\n inside JSON string.\n\nCareful with quotes inside notes: notes quote words in single quotes — fine in JSON.\n\nLet me write rows in order n ascending as they appear: 8,9,18,20,22,26,27,28,29,33,38,39,40,47,51,57.\n\nCorrections:\n\nn=22 sop:\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"I\"\n@c1 syntax.clause form=declarative verb=\"keep\" subject=$n1\n@c2 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"missing\" complement=$n2\n@n2 syntax.nominal dependent=\"bin\" head=\"days\"\n@k1 syntax.coordination conjunction=\"so\" conjunct=$c1 conjunct=$c3\n@c3 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"let\" complement=$n3\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"'s\"\n@c4 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"have\" complement=$n4\n@n4 syntax.nominal dependent=\"a\" dependent=\"Chores\" head=\"calendar\"\n@n5 syntax.nominal dependent=\"bright\" head=\"magenta\"\n@r1 syntax.relation from=$c4 to=$n5\n@n6 syntax.nominal head=\"it\"\n@c5 syntax.clause form=declarative verb=\"stands\" modifier=\"out\" subject=$n6\n@r2 syntax.relation marker=\"so\" from=$c4 to=$c5\n@r3 syntax.relation from=$c1 to=$c2\n@r4 syntax.relation from=$c3 to=$c4\n\nWait \"'s\" — in JSON: head=\\\"'s\\\" — fine.\n\nn=39 (\\(\\ne11\\)) correction as drafted.\n\nn=47 correction as drafted.\n\nn=57 correction:\n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative discourse=\"ok\" discourse=\"so\" verb=\"paint\" modifier=\"black\" complement=$n1\n@k1 syntax.coordination conjunction=\"and\" conjunct=$c1 conjunct=$c2\n@n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" dependent=\"Gym\" head=\"calendar\"\n@c2 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"move\" complement=$n2\n@n2 syntax.nominal head=\"yoga\"\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"onto\" from=$c2 to=$n3\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"it\"\n\nGood. Now write part 1. Note the \"so\" as discourse: rules allow discourse for \"So\" at the start.\n\nLet me now do the bash append.",
