    "text": "n=72: alt uses k1 coordination with conjunction \"so\" — good; so alt is right → use as corrected sop.\n\nn=99: alt writes \"needs\" as verb of c1, \"adding\" as nonfinite clause tied to c1. That's the right reading. So the sop's aux=needs/verb=adding is a wrong statement. Grade: L1 (\"Its verb is a word that is no verb here, or an auxiliary while the main verb stands elsewhere\" — here the main verb \"needs\" stands in the auxiliary slot, and the gerund occupies the verb slot of a declarative clause claiming Hannah adds). I'll grade L1 with note quoting 'Hannah needs adding', and use alt as the corrected sop.\n\nHmm, is L1 vs L8 fair? Let me consider the \"next layer\" test again: circuit says one clause, aux \"needs\", verb \"adding\", subject Hannah. \"Hannah needs adding\" — the next layer would interpret the aspect/aux wrongly; \"needs\" is the main verb and not an auxiliary. The rule set says aux words must be right; a non-auxiliary word held as an auxiliary with the main verb displaced is the same kind of fault L1 names (\"an auxiliary while the main verb stands elsewhere\" — the mirror). I'll go L1.\n\nn=109: alt writes k1 \"so\" joining c1 and c2, with \"Mihai\" as a dependent of \"person\". So alt is right → use as correction. Note: alt holds \"Mihai\" as dependent of \"the person\" nominal — allowed by the case \"A number or a name right after a noun ... may be a dependent in that noun's nominal, or a nominal of its own tied to the noun\". Good.\n\nn=113: alt: n2 head=\"Tom wrong\" — hmm, \"Tom wrong\" as head of a nominal — is that OK? Words \"Tom wrong\" in one head is odd but a multi-word head is allowed for names (\"Team lunch\"). Hmm, \"Tom wrong\" — the rule \"A name of several words may stand whole in head\". \"Tom wrong\" isn't a name. And \"wrong\" held in head as part of the nominal loses \"wrong\" as describing how the spelling went → L3-ish (words of a nominal merged / head wrong). Also alt's k1 joins c1 and c2 correctly. And r1 from c1 to c3 ties the question.\n\nI'd rather write my own correction for n=113 (my version above with modifier \"wrong\"). Both alt and mine are plausible; mine keeps coordination c1+c2 and a relation c2→c3. Let me use my own version.\n\nNow write part 2: rows 16-46 (n=58,59,60,62,63,66,68,72,75,77,78,81,83,86,88,90,91,93,99,100,101,107,108,109,111,113,114,115,117,123,127).\n\nGrades:\nn=58 L8\nn=59 L8\nn=60 L1 (verb Family)\nn=62 L1 (fragment, colour as noun)\nn=63 L8\nn=66 L8\nn=68 L8\nn=72 L3\nn=75 L8\nn=77 L8\nn=78 L8\nn=81 L8\nn=83 L8\nn=86 L8\nn=88 L8\nn=90 L8\nn=91 L8\nn=93 L8\nn=99 L1\nn=100 L8\nn=101 L8\nn=107 L8\nn=108 L8\nn=109 L3\nn=111 L8\nn=113 L3\nn=114 L8\nn=115 L8\nn=117 L8\nn=123 L8\nn=127 L8\n\nCorrections:\nn=60: fragment.\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"Family\"\n@n2 syntax.nominal dependent=\"a\" head=\"bit\"\n@c1 syntax.clause form=fragment modifier=\"darker\" complement=$n1 complement=$n2\n\nn=62:\n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"colour\" modifier=\"gold\" complement=$n1\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"it\"\n\nn=72: alt.\nn=99: alt.\nn=109: alt.\nn=113: mine.\n\nLet me append.",
