    "text": "Judge rows 47-74.\n\nRow 47 (n=128): \"I want Lee removed as a person\"\nsop: c1 declarative want subject I; n2 Lee; c2 nonfinite removed subject n2; r1 marker=as from c2 to n3(a person); r2 c1→c2. Good. L8.\n\nRow 48 (n=130): \"Lee left the company on Friday, so please remove him from my people\"\nsop: c1 left subject Lee complement the company; r1 marker=on from c1 to n3(Friday); k1 so c1 c2; c2 imperative please remove complement him; r2 from c2 to n5(my people). Good. L8.\n\nRow 49 (n=131): \"I added Gary by mistake last week. Could you delete him?\"\nsop: c1 declarative added subject I complement Gary complement n4(last week); r1 marker=by from c1 to n3(mistake); c2 interrogative Could delete subject you complement him. Good. L8.\n\nRow 50 (n=132): \"rename Priya to Priya Nair and delete the duplicate Priya N.\" Good. L8.\n\nRow 51 (n=134): \"our contractor Olga finished her project; would you mind deleting her contact?\"\nsop: n1 \"our contractor\"; n2 Olga; c1 finished subject n1 complement n3; c2 interrogative would mind subject you; c3 nonfinite deleting complement n5; r1 from n1 to n2; k1 coordination c1 c2; r2 c2→c3. Good. L8.\n\nRow 52 (n=135): \"no more Lee\" — fragment. L8.\n\nRow 53 (n=143): \"facilities just opened a quiet space, so please add it as a room called Quiet corner\"\nsop: c1 declarative modifier=just verb=opened subject=facilities complement=n2; k1 so c1 c2; c2 imperative please add complement it; r1 marker=as from c2 to n4(a room); c3 nonfinite called complement Quiet corner; r2 from n4 to c3. Good. L8.\n\nRow 54 (n=144): \"add a room named Atrium and then book it for the design critique\" — good. L8.\n\nRow 55 (n=149): \"create a meeting room, Harbour, that fits 8 people\"\nsop: c1 imperative create complement n1; n2 Harbour; r1 marker=that from n1 to c2; c2 declarative fits complement n3(8 people) — no subject! \"that fits 8 people\": the subject is \"that\" (the relative pronoun, which is the marker of r1). c2 has no subject but that's the marker. Fine. r2 n1→n2. L8.\n\nRow 56 (n=150): \"we converted the old storeroom into a small huddle space - could you add it as Nook, it seats four?\"\nsop: c1 declarative converted subject we complement n2; r1 into from c1 to n3; c2 interrogative could add subject you complement it; r2 as from c2 to n6(Nook); n7 it; c3 form=interrogative verb=seats subject=it complement=four; r3 from c3 to c1; r4 from c3 to c2.\n\nProblem: \"it seats four?\" — is that a question? The message: \"we converted the old storeroom into a small huddle space - could you add it as Nook, it seats four?\" The trailing \"it seats four?\" is a statement (a tag/appositive) with a question mark at the end, but the question mark belongs to the whole sentence/question. \"it seats four\" is a declarative clause (subject it, verb seats). Written as interrogative → L1? Per rules: \"a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\". \"it seats four\" is not a clear question; it's a statement about the room (with the sentence's question mark). Hmm — but \"it seats four?\" is arguably a question (\"does it seat four?\"). Hmm. In speech, \"it seats four?\" after a question mark ends the sentence... The final \"?\" ends the whole clause \"could you add it as Nook, it seats four?\" A reader would take \"it seats four\" as a statement of fact, not a question. Since it's declarative in form (subject+verb, no auxiliary fronting, no question word), writing it as interrogative is the \"reverse\" error → L1.\n\nHmm, careful. The message: \"could you add it as Nook, it seats four?\" Reading as one padded request. \"it seats four\" — no inversion, so it's declarative in form. Grade L1? Or L8 (plausible reading)? The next layer reading c3 as interrogative would think it asks \"does it seat four?\" — a statement turned into a question, which is a different claim about the message. Hmm, but the whole message ends with \"?\" and both the request and this clause share it.\n\nI think per the settled-case style (\"A clause that opens with a question word inside another clause states: its form is declarative... Written as interrogative it is L1\"), the mirror case (a declarative-in-form clause written interrogative) is L1. Here the clause has no inversion and no question word, so it cannot be a question. I'll grade L1.\n\nHmm, but hold on. Is \"it seats four?\" maybe a separate question following the request? \"could you add it as Nook, it seats four?\" — one question mark at the very end covers both. Grammatically \"it seats four\" is declarative. I'll go L1 with a corrected sop: c3 form=declarative verb=seats subject=it complement=four, tied to c2 (or c1).\n\nWait, also r3/r4 tie c3 to both c1 and c2 — fine.\n\nAlso \"four\" as complement — fine.\n\nCorrection: same as sop but c3 form=declarative. Use alt if right? Let me check alt for 150 later. I'll write my own:\n@n1 nominal head=\"we\"\n@c1 declarative verb=\"converted\" subject=$n1 complement=$n2\n@n2 nominal dependent=\"the\" dependent=\"old\" head=\"storeroom\"\n@r1 relation marker=\"into\" from=$c1 to=$n3\n@n3 nominal dependent=\"a\" dependent=\"small\" dependent=\"huddle\" head=\"space\"\n@c2 interrogative auxiliary=\"could\" verb=\"add\" subject=$n4 complement=$n5\n@n4 nominal head=\"you\"\n@n5 nominal head=\"it\"\n@r2 relation marker=\"as\" from=$c2 to=$n6\n@n6 nominal head=\"Nook\"\n@n7 nominal head=\"it\"\n@c3 declarative verb=\"seats\" subject=$n7 complement=$n8\n@n8 nominal head=\"four\"\n@r3 relation from=$c2 to=$c3\n\nRow 57 (n=151): \"room for 6 called Birch\" — fragment: n1 room, r1 for n1→n2(6), c1 nonfinite called complement Birch, c2 fragment complement n1, r2 n1→c1. Good. L8.\n\nRow 58 (n=153): \"new 10-seat room please\" — n1 deps new,10,seat head room — hmm \"10-seat\" one nominal head \"room\". Fine. L8.\n\nRow 59 (n=155): \"create a room named Studio B in building B\" — good. L8. \"Studio B\": dep Studio head B — fine.\n\nRow 60 (n=156): \"please add the Garden room, which is on the ground floor\"\nsop: c1 imperative please add complement n1; r1 marker=which from n1 to c2; c2 declarative verb=is (no complement!); r2 marker=on from c2 to n2(the ground floor).\n\"which is on the ground floor\": \"is\" verb, \"on the ground floor\" related via r2 from c2. Fine. c2 has no subject (the marker \"which\" is the subject). OK. L8.\n\nRow 61 (n=157): \"I'd like a new room, Lighthouse, located in the east wing\"\nsop: c1 declarative 'd like subject I complement n2(a new room); n3 Lighthouse; c2 nonfinite located; r1 marker=in from c2 to n4; r2 n2→n3; r3 n2→c2. Good. L8.\n\nRow 62 (n=159): \"set up a room, Orion, at the Cluj office\" — good. L8.\n\nRow 63 (n=160): \"new meeting room Hazel, location: level 2, by the lifts\"\nsop: n1 new meeting room; n2 Hazel; n3 location; n4 level; n5 2; r1 marker=by attachment=ambiguous from c1 to n6(the lifts); c1 fragment complement n1; r2 n1→n2; r3 n1→n3; r4 n3→n4; r5 n4→n5.\nAll words: new meeting room Hazel location level 2 by the lifts. Covered: n1 covers new/meeting/room; n2 Hazel; n3 location; n4 level; n5 2; \"by\" marker; n6 the lifts. Good. L8.\n\nRow 64 (n=161): \"register a room called Summit up on the 7th floor\"\nsop: c1 imperative register modifier=up complement n1; c2 nonfinite called complement Summit; r1 marker=on attachment=ambiguous from c1 to n3(the 7th floor); r2 n1→c2. Good. L8.\n\nRow 65 (n=162): \"we rented extra space downtown, so could you add a room called Annex at 12 Market Street?\"\nsop: c1 declarative rented modifier=downtown subject we complement n2; k1 so c1 c2; c2 interrogative could add subject you complement n4(a room); c3 nonfinite called complement Annex; r1 marker=at from c2 to n6(12 Market Street); r2 n4→c3. Good. L8.\n\nRow 66 (n=163): \"visitors always get lost, so when you add the Welcome room please note it's right by reception\"\nsop: c1 declarative modifier=always aux=get verb=lost subject=visitors; r1 marker=\"so when\" from c1 to c2; c2 declarative verb=add subject=you complement n3; c3 imperative please note; n4 it; c4 declarative verb=\"'s\" modifier=right subject=it; r2 marker=by from c4 to n5(reception); k1 coordination c1 c3; r3 c3→c4.\n\nIssues:\n- r1 marker=\"so when\" — a marker should be a linking word; \"so when\" is two words as one marker. Rules: \"A relation has ... a `marker` that is no linking word\". Hmm, \"so\" and \"when\" — two linking words held as one marker. Is that a wrong statement? Words must each stand in exactly one value; both \"so\" and \"when\" are in the marker. It's a merged marker. Hmm, there's no rule explicitly about a two-word marker. \"right on\" was allowed as a marker holding the linking word plus a grading word. \"so when\" is different: \"so\" coordinates the whole sentence and \"when\" marks the subordinate clause. Merging them into one marker loses the \"so\" tie (c1 → c2/c3 coordination). Is that \"lost information\"? The information that \"so\" joins the visitors clause with the request is lost — the merged marker \"so when\" from c1 to c2 kind of conveys it.\n- More importantly: \"please note it's right by reception\" — c3 imperative note with complement...? c3 has no complement; n4 \"it\" is the subject of c4. \"note\" takes the clause c4 as complement; r3 ties c3→c4. OK.\n- k1 coordination: conjunct c1 and c3 (the request \"note...\") — \"so\" is used as marker of r1 (from c1 to c2 \"when you add...\"). Hmm, so \"so\" appears both in the marker and joins c1 and c3 via k1 without a conjunction. Redundant but each way is a plausible reading? Actually k1 has no conjunction — side-by-side join of \"visitors always get lost\" and \"please note...\". And r1 with marker \"so when\" from c1 to c2 — c2 is \"when you add the Welcome room\", which is not an end...  wait, \"when you add the Welcome room\" — the \"when\" clause modifies c3 (\"please note it's right by reception\"). Starting that relation at c1 (the visitors clause) is odd: \"when you add the Welcome room\" belongs with \"please note\", not with \"visitors always get lost\".\n\nHmm, so r1 from c1 to c2 says \"visitors always get lost ... when you add the Welcome room\" — that's a wrong tie: the \"when\" clause hangs on the request. Per L3: \"A relation ... has a start the words rule out\". Is it ruled out? \"so when you add the Welcome room, please note...\" — the reading is \"so, when you add the Welcome room, note that...\". \"when\" clause attaches to \"note\". Starting at c1 crosses the coordination... but grammatically \"when you add the Welcome room\" could conceivably modify \"visitors always get lost\"? No — it stands right before \"please note\", i.e., in the second part. So the start is wrong → L3? Modest but real.\n\nAlso is c2 tied properly otherwise? c2 is the end of r1. c3 is a conjunct of k1. c3's relation to c2? None; r3 ties c3→c4.\n\nHmm, does the circuit \"state what the message does not say\"? r1 says c1 (visitors get lost) is tied to \"when you add the Welcome room\" by \"so when\" — combining.\n\nAlternatively consider: is the merge \"so when\" a marker that is no linking word? Actually each is a linking word.\n\nI think this is a genuine L3: the \"when\" clause belongs with the request clause, and the circuit ties it to \"visitors always get lost\" while also writing the \"so\" tie twice.\n\nHmm, but let me be careful about being too strict. What would a next layer do? It reads: \"visitors always get lost\" --so when--> \"you add the Welcome room\", and separately c1 k1 c3 (\"please note\"), and c3→c4 (\"it's right by reception\"). It can more or less reconstruct. The \"so when\" merged marker is unusual. Honestly the analysis \"when you add the Welcome room\" starting at c1 is defensible?! In \"visitors always get lost, so when you add the Welcome room please note it's right by reception\" — \"so\" joins the two sentences; \"when...\" is inside the second. So the relation marked \"when\" starting at c1 (the first sentence) is wrong: the when-clause is in the second sentence.\n\nI'll grade L3 with note quoting 'so when you add the Welcome room please note it's right by reception'. Correction: split: r1 marker=\"so\" from c1 to c3; r2 marker=\"when\" from c3 to c2; c2 declarative verb=add subject=you complement=the Welcome room; c3 imperative please note; c4 declarative 's ... \n\nCorrection circuit:\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"visitors\"\n@c1 syntax.clause form=declarative modifier=\"always\" auxiliary=\"get\" verb=\"lost\" subject=$n1\n@n2 syntax.nominal head=\"you\"\n@c2 syntax.clause form=declarative verb=\"add\" subject=$n2 complement=$n3\n@n3 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" dependent=\"Welcome\" head=\"room\"\n@c3 syntax.clause form=imperative discourse=\"please\" verb=\"note\"\n@n4 syntax.nominal head=\"it\"\n@c4 syntax.clause form=declarative verb=\"'s\" modifier=\"right\" subject=$n4\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"by\" from=$c4 to=$n5\n@n5 syntax.nominal head=\"reception\"\n@r2 syntax.relation marker=\"when\" from=$c3 to=$c2\n@r3 syntax.relation marker=\"so\" from=$c1 to=$c3\n@r4 syntax.relation from=$c3 to=$c4\n\nAll words: visitors always get lost so when you add the Welcome room please note it 's right by reception. Covered. Good.\n\nRow 67 (n=167): \"room on the 2nd floor called Fern\" — like row 57. L8.\n\nRow 68 (n=168): \"add a room upstairs\" — c1 imperative add modifier=upstairs complement a room. Fine (\"up\" as modifier per settled case). L8.\n\nRow 69 (n=170): \"add Jade room, 4th floor\" — c1 imperative add complement n1(Jade room); n2(4th floor); r1 n1→n2. Hmm, n2 dangling? It's the end of r1. Fine. L8.\n\nRow 70 (n=171): \"a room near the entrance, call it Porch\"\nsop: n1 a room; r1 marker=near from n1 to n2; c1 imperative call complement it complement Porch; c2 fragment complement n1; k1 coordination c2 c1. Good. L8.\n\nRow 71 (n=174): \"new room downstairs: Cellar\" — c1 fragment modifier=downstairs complement n1; n2 Cellar; r1 n1→n2. Good. L8.\n\nRow 72 (n=176): \"call the board room the 