    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "Judge rows 102-123.\n\nRow 102 (n=245): \"ok so nobody ever books the Focus pod; let's just delete it\"\nsop: c1 declarative discourse=ok modifier=so modifier=ever verb=books subject=nobody complement=n2; c2 imperative verb=let complement=n3('s); c3 nonfinite modifier=just verb=delete complement=it; k1 coordination c1 c2; r1 c2→c3. Good. L8.\n\nRow 103 (n=247): \"no more Cedar room\" — fragment. L8.\n\nRow 104 (n=250): \"Board room - gone\"\nsop: n1 Board room; c1 nonfinite verb=gone subject=n1.\n\"Board room - gone\": a fragment (\"Board room gone\") — no finite verb; \"gone\" is a participle. Written as a nonfinite clause with subject — accepted per my leniency policy? It's a message that is a fragment; the circuit writes one nonfinite clause with subject n1. Nothing lost, tied internally (subject). Hmm, L6 (participle tied to nothing)? It's the whole message — \"Separate sentences need no tie\"; the clause is the message. Fine, L8.\n\nRow 105 (n=256): \"I need a meeting with Lee, Priya and Ana next Tuesday at 11\"\nsop: c1 need; r1 with from n2(meeting) to k1; k1 and: Lee, Priya, Ana; n6 next Tuesday; r2 at from c1 to n7(11); r3 from n2 to n6. Good. L8.\n\nRow 106 (n=257): \"please arrange a planning session with Mihai and Priya on Dec 3 at 3pm\"\nsop: c1 arrange; r1 with from n1 to k1; k1 Mihai, Priya; r2 on from n1 to n4(Dec); r3 at from n1 to n6(3pm); r4 from n4 to n5(3). Good. L8.\n\nRow 107 (n=260): \"create a meeting called Hiring debrief with Priya and Ana on the 24th at 1pm\"\nsop: c1 create complement n1(meeting); c2 nonfinite called; c3 nonfinite verb=Hiring complement=n2(debrief); r1 with from n1 to k1(Priya and Ana); r2 on from c1 to n5(the 24th); r3 at from c1 to n6(1pm); r4 n1→c2; r5 c2→c3.\nFault: \"Hiring debrief\" — the name split into two clauses: c3 verb=\"Hiring\" (a noun/gerund made the verb of a nonfinite clause!) with complement \"debrief\". So \"Hiring\" is made a verb — L1 (\"Its verb is a word that is no verb here\"; \"A noun made its verb is L1\"). And \"Hiring debrief\" as a name is split into two nominals instead of one.\nGrade L1? The clause c3's verb is \"Hiring\" — a noun. L1: \"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here\". Yes L1.\n\nCorrection: n_name = nominal dependent=\"Hiring\" head=\"debrief\" tied to \"meeting\" via the nonfinite \"called\":\n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"create\" complement=$n1\n@n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"a\" head=\"meeting\"\n@c2 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"called\" complement=$n2\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"with\" from=$n1 to=$k1\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"Priya\"\n@k1 syntax.coordination conjunction=\"and\" conjunct=$n3 conjunct=$n4\n@n4 syntax.nominal head=\"Ana\"\n@r2 syntax.relation attachment=ambiguous marker=\"on\" from=$c1 to=$n5\n@n5 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" head=\"24th\"\n@r3 syntax.relation attachment=ambiguous marker=\"at\" from=$c1 to=$n6\n@n6 syntax.nominal head=\"1pm\"\n@r4 syntax.relation from=$n1 to=$c2\n@n2 syntax.nominal dependent=\"Hiring\" head=\"debrief\"\n\nRow 108 (n=262): \"ok so Ana's back from leave Monday. Book a catch-up with her at 10, please\"\nsop: c1 declarative discourse=ok modifier=so verb=\"'s\" modifier=back subject=n1(Ana) complement=n3(Monday); r1 from c1 to n2(leave) marker=from; c2 imperative Book please complement n4; r2 with n4→n5(her); r3 at from c2 to n6(10).\nGood. \"back\" as modifier — fine. L8.\n\nRow 109 (n=263): \"if Mihai and Lee are both around tomorrow morning, put a quick sync with them in the calendar\"\nsop: r1 marker=if from c2 to c1; n1 Mihai; k1 and conjunct n1 n2; c1 declarative verb=are modifier=around subject=k1 complement=n3(both) complement=n4(tomorrow morning); c2 imperative put complement n5; r2 with n5→n6; r3 in from c2 to n7.\n\"both\" as a complement nominal of c1 — odd but fine. L8.\n\nRow 110 (n=265): \"I'd love to get Ana, Lee and Mihai in a room to talk about the offsite - could you find a time on Wednesday?\"\nsop: c1 'd love; r1 to from c1 to c2; c2 nonfinite get complement k1; k1 and: Ana, Lee, Mihai; r2 in from c2 to n5(a room); r3 to from c2 to c3(talk); r4 about from c3 to n6(the offsite); c4 interrogative could find subject you complement n8(a time); r5 on from c4 to n9(Wednesday); k2 coordination c1 c4. Good. L8.\n\nRow 111 (n=266): \"would you mind booking an hour with Lee next Monday at 3? He asked to review the contract\"\nsop: c1 interrogative would mind subject you; c2 nonfinite booking complement n2(an hour) complement n4(next Monday); r1 with c2→n3(Lee); r2 at from c2 to n5(3); n6 He; c3 declarative asked subject He; r3 to from c3 to c4; c4 nonfinite review complement n7(the contract); r4 c1→c2. Good. L8.\n\nRow 112 (n=268): \"a call with Mihai sometime this week\" — fragment; r1 with n1→n2; n3 sometime this week; r2 n1→n3. L8.\n\nRow 113 (n=276): \"the client can only do mornings, so let's put the Kickoff meeting on Wednesday at 8:30\"\nsop: c1 can only do mornings; k1 so c1 c2; c2 declarative verb=let complement n3('s); c3 nonfinite put complement n4; r1 on from c3 to n5(Wednesday); r2 at from c3 to n6(8:30); r3 c2→c3.\nFine per settled case for let's (c2 imperative or declarative both pass? The case says \"let\" is the verb; \"'s\" a nominal complement; \"may be the subject of the nonfinite clause of add\". And \"'s' or 'us' as the subject of 'let', or 'add' written as a declarative clause, fails: L1\". Hmm! Here c3 \"put\" is nonfinite (not declarative) ✓; c2 \"let\" is written form=declarative — the failing case is \"'add' written as a declarative clause\" (the clause after let). c2 is the \"let\" clause; writing it declarative instead of imperative — the case says holding \"'s\"/\"us\" as subject of \"let\" fails, and \"add\" as declarative fails. It doesn't say \"let\" as declarative fails. Hmm, \"let's put...\" is an imperative. Writing it declarative (form) — for the case, the failing form error is on the following verb. I'd accept L8: the reading is complete, \"let\" is the verb, \"'s\" complement, \"put\" nonfinite tied.\n\nRow 114 (n=277): \"I think the retro should happen right after the sprint ends - could you schedule a Retro on the 28th at 4pm with Lee?\"\nsop: c1 think subject I; c2 declarative should happen modifier=right subject=the retro; r1 marker=after from c2 to c3; c3 declarative ends subject the sprint; c4 interrogative could schedule subject you complement n5(a Retro); r2 on from c4 to n6; r3 at from c4 to n7; r4 with from c4 to n8; r5 c1→c2; r6 c4→c1. Good. L8.\n\nRow 115 (n=278): \"meeting tomorrow at 2\"\nsop: c1 form=nonfinite verb=\"meeting\" modifier=tomorrow; r1 at from c1 to n1(2).\nFault: \"meeting\" is not a verb, and the message has no verb → should be a fragment. c1 nonfinite with verb=\"meeting\" (a noun made the verb) → L1 (\"A noun made its verb is L1\"; also form: no-verb part should be fragment).\n\nCorrection:\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"meeting\"\n@c1 syntax.clause form=fragment modifier=\"tomorrow\" complement=$n1\n@r1 syntax.relation attachment=ambiguous marker=\"at\" from=$c1 to=$n2\n@n2 syntax.nominal head=\"2\"\n\nRow 116 (n=279): \"Ops review Monday morning\" — fragment n1(Ops review), n2(Monday morning), r1 n1→n2. L8.\n\nRow 117 (n=282): \"could you squeeze in a 15-minute check-in with Priya before lunch tomorrow? Nothing longer, she's busy\"\nsop: c1 interrogative could squeeze modifier=in subject=you complement=n2; r1 with n2→n3; r2 before from c1 to n4(lunch, dep tomorrow); n5 Nothing; c2 fragment modifier=longer complement=n5; n6 she; c3 declarative verb='s modifier=busy subject=she; k1 coordination c2 c3.\n\"Nothing longer\" — fragment with \"longer\" as modifier ✓ (per settled case: word after nominal describing → modifier of clause). \"she's busy\" declarative ✓. k1 joins c2 and c3 — the sentences \"Nothing longer, she's busy\" — fine. L8.\n\nRow 118 (n=284): \"hour-long meeting with Ana on Thursday\" — fragment. L8.\n\nRow 119 (n=285): \"block 3 hours for a Planning offsite\"\nsop: n1 head=\"block\"; n2 \"3 hours\"; r1 marker=for from c1 to n3; c1 fragment complement=n1; r2 from n1 to n2.\nFault: is \"block\" a verb here? \"block 3 hours for a Planning offsite\" — \"block\" can be a verb (to block time) or a noun. Per settled case: \"A command needs its verb. When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb ('book design review', 'schedule the kickoff'), it is the verb; held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1.\"\n\nHmm! \"block 3 hours for a Planning offsite\" — \"block\" is the first word, can be a verb, and the rest \"3 hours for a Planning offsite\" reads as its object. So writing \"block\" as a noun in a fragment loses the command → L1.\n\nCareful: is there another verb in the message? \"Planning\" is a noun-ish. So no other verb. So yes L1.\n\nHmm, but \"block\" as a noun with \"3 hours\" as a nominal: reading \"block 3 hours\" as a noun+noun? Eh. The settled case is explicit about this pattern. L1.\n\nCorrection:\n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"block\" complement=$n1\n@n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"3\" head=\"hours\"\n@r1 syntax.relation attachment=ambiguous marker=\"for\" from=$c1 to=$n2\n@n2 syntax.nominal dependent=\"a\" dependent=\"Planning\" head=\"offsite\"\n\nRow 120 (n=289): \"I need somewhere quiet for my interview with Priya on Wednesday at 11 - the Focus pod would be perfect\"\nsop: c1 need modifier=somewhere modifier=quiet subject I; r1 for from c1 to n2; r2 with n2→n3; r3 on n2→n4(Wednesday); r4 at from n4 to n5(11); c2 declarative would be modifier=perfect subject=n6; r5 c2→c1. Good. L8.\n\nRow 121 (n=291): \"meeting with Mihai, Cedar room\"\nsop: c1 nonfinite verb=\"meeting\" complement=n2(Cedar room); r1 with c1→n1.\nSame issue as n=278: \"meeting\" is not a verb; message has no verb → fragment. L1 (\"a noun made its verb\").\n\nCorrection:\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"Mihai\"\n@n2 syntax.nominal dependent=\"Cedar\" head=\"room\"\n@c1 syntax.clause form=fragment complement=$n2\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"with\" from=$c1 to=$n1\n\nRow 122 (n=296): \"Ana and I are planning the holiday party; can you create a Party planning meeting with her on the Work calendar for next Monday at 4?\"\nsop: n1 Ana; k1 and; n2 I; c1 declarative are planning subject=k1 complement=n3; c2 interrogative can create subject you complement n5; r1 with n5→n6(her); r2 on from c2 to n7(the Work calendar); r3 for from c2 to n8(next Monday); r4 at from n8 to n9(4); k2 coordination c1 c2. Good. L8.\n\nRow 123 (n=297): \"meeting on the Work calendar tomorrow\"\nsop: c1 nonfinite verb=\"meeting\" modifier=tomorrow; r1 on from c1 to n1.\nSame fault (noun as verb) → L1.\n\nCorrection:\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"meeting\"\n@c1 syntax.clause form=fragment modifier=\"tomorrow\" complement=$n1\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"on\" from=$c1 to=$n2\n@n2 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" dependent=\"Work\" head=\"calendar\"\n\nGood. Note: three rows (278, 291, 297) share the \"meeting\" as verb fault; n=250 \"Board room - gone\" was fine because \"gone\" is a participle.\n\nNow, wait: n=250 \"Board room - gone\": c1 nonfinite verb=gone subject=n1 — \"gone\" is a verb form (participle) so it's fine as a nonfinite verb. ✓.\n\nLet me write part 5 (rows 102-123, 22 rows) and then the last chunk (rows 124-149, 26 rows).\n\nRejections here: n=260 (L1), n=278 (L1), n=285 (L1), n=291 (L1), n=297 (L1).\n\nCareful: appending duplicated the previous block once (bash oddity). I'll append and then check for duplicates with dedupe+count.",
