    "text": "Judge rows 124-139.\n\nRow 124 (n=298): \"family meeting Sunday, family calendar\" — c1 fragment complement n1(family meeting) complement n3(family calendar); r1 n1→n2(Sunday). Good. L8.\n\nRow 125 (n=299): \"add a Gym meeting with coach Dan\" — L8.\n\nRow 126 (n=300): \"schedule a meeting with Ana on Monday at 10 with the note bring the Q3 figures\"\nsop: c1 schedule complement n1; r1 with n1→n2; r2 on n1→n3; r3 at from c1 to n4; r4 with from c1 to n5(the note); c2 imperative verb=bring complement n6(the Q3 figures); k1 coordination c1 c2.\nIssue: \"bring the Q3 figures\" is the content of the note (a command inside a note). Held as an imperative clause coordinated with c1 — per the settled case: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3\". Hmm, the rule is about a clause that ends a relation with a marker standing inside another clause. Here \"bring the Q3 figures\" would be the content of \"the note\". Written as an imperative clause joined in coordination with the main command — that states two commands side by side, losing that \"bring the Q3 figures\" is the note's content.\n\nHmm. But is it \"lost\"? The note is tied by r4 to c1, and c2 is a separate command. The next layer sees: schedule a meeting with the note; bring the Q3 figures (a command). Similar to the \"saying/note\" cases where the rules demanded content be a clause tied to the word of saying. Here c2 is tied to nothing (only k1 coordination). Hmm.\n\nThe settled case for content: \"Words said to the listener that are mentioned as content ('a paragraph saying Thank you', 'add the paragraph Please review') are that content: a nominal, or a clause tied to the word of saying. Held as discourse, the content is lost: L2.\" So content should be a clause tied to the word of saying — here c2 (\"bring the Q3 figures\") is not tied to \"the note\" (n5); it's a conjunct of k1 with c1. So the content of the note is not tied to the note → the tie is missing → L6? or L3 (wrong tie)?\n\nHmm. Here two commands side by side is a plausible \"incomplete reading\": the next layer can see the note and the imperative. But strictly the note's content is tied to nothing.\n\nLet me weigh: which of these note-content rows (126, 127, 300s...) look wrong? Rows 127, 131, 132, 135, 137 use k1 with fragments/imperatives similarly. Let me examine each:\n\nRow 127 (n=301): \"book a call with Lee tomorrow at 2pm, notes: discuss the vendor contract\"\nsop: c1 book complement n1; r1 with n1→n2; n3 tomorrow; r2 at from c1 to n4; n5 \"notes\"; c2 imperative discuss complement n6; k1 coordination conjunct=c1, c3, c2; r3 n1→n3; c3 fragment complement=n5(notes).\nSo \"notes\" is a fragment c3, and \"discuss the vendor contract\" an imperative c2; all three conjuncts of one coordination. Again: content of notes not tied to \"notes\"; and a coordination with three conjuncts including a fragment.\n\nRow 131 (n=305): \"put a Supplier call on Dec 3 at 4pm, note: ask about delivery dates\"\nsop: c1 put; ... c2 imperative verb=note (!); c3 imperative ask complement? no; r3 about from c3 to n5; k1 coordination c1 c2 c3.\nHere \"note\" is written as a verb of an imperative clause — \"note:\" as a label for the note. Hmm, so the message is read as \"put ..., note: ask about delivery dates\" → \"note\" a verb (imperative) and \"ask about delivery dates\" another imperative. That's a plausible reading? The words \"note:\" is a heading for the note text \"ask about delivery dates\". Reading \"note\" as a verb command (\"note: ask about...\") is odd but... Actually grammatically \"note:\" followed by content — \"note\" is a noun label. Making it a verb: L1? \"Its verb is a word that is no verb here\" — \"note\" can be a verb. Hmm.\n\nThis is getting deep. Let me step back and define a consistent, defensible treatment for the \"notes/agenda content\" rows:\n\nThe messages: \"schedule a meeting ... with the note bring the Q3 figures\" (280?), \"book a call ..., notes: discuss the vendor contract\" (301), \"create a Hiring sync ... and add the note Review the three shortlisted CVs\" (302), \"can you set up ... and write in the notes that we'll go through the Q4 roadmap?\" (303), \"please add a meeting ... with the agenda Budget overrun and next steps\" (304), \"put a Supplier call ..., note: ask about delivery dates\" (305), \"I'd like a meeting ...; in the notes put Bring laptops for the demo\" (306), \"schedule Onboarding ... and attach the note Dial-in code 4471\" (307), \"Priya always forgets the slides, so when you book our Monday 11am meeting, add a note saying Priya brings the slides\" (308), \"set up a call ... - and could you jot down in the notes: confirm the hotel booking\" (309), \"Please put that in, with the note that he wants the competitor analysis first\" (310), \"book a Design sync ... with notes Share Figma link, and cancel the yoga\" (311), \"add a meeting with Priya and note that it's about the parental leave handover\" (312), \"Notes should say: print 10 copies of the deck\" (313).\n\nFor these, the natural correct circuit ties the content clause to the word \"note\"/\"notes\"/\"agenda\" via a relation (marker or no marker) — as the settled case demands (\"a clause tied to the word of saying\").\n\nSo the question: does each sop tie the content to \"note\"? Let's check:\n- 300: content c2 imperative NOT tied to n5 (the note) → k1 only. Fault. Which grade? The settled case's grade for content in discourse is L2; here it's not discourse. The nearest: L6 (\"A pairing the words give is not written\") or L3 (\"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\"). Hmm, c2 as a conjunct of a coordination between two commands (\"schedule ...\" and \"bring ...\") states a second command the message doesn't give → that's \"a coordination ... between wires that the message does not relate\" (L4) or wrong conjunct (L3).\n\nHmm. Honestly, for 300, \"schedule a meeting with Ana ... with the note bring the Q3 figures\" — \"bring the Q3 figures\" is the note's text. Writing it as an imperative command coordinated with the main command is a wrong statement (the message does not command it) → L3 (wrong conjunct) or L4.\n\nBut is it \"plausible\"? A next layer reading \"c2 imperative bring the Q3 figures\" as the note content could infer from the coordination... it's sloppy but recoverable. The rules are strict about \"a command cannot stand inside another clause\". Here the command doesn't stand inside another clause — it stands as a conjunct. Reading the coordination c1+c2 as \"the note's content joined to the command\" is a stretch but the next layer's job is meaning; it would see two commands: \"schedule...\" and \"bring the Q3 figures\" and would have to undo: no, the message doesn't ask you to bring figures; it says to put that in a note. That's a wrong statement to undo → reject.\n\nHmm, but the formalizer's intent might be exactly \"the note content\". Since the tie is missing, I'd reject as L6 (a pairing the words give is not written: the content not tied to the note) — hmm, L6 is \"Something is left untied. A clause or a nominal is tied to nothing inside its sentence.\" c2 is tied (by k1)... \n\nI need to decide a policy. Let me use the settled case pattern for reported content: \"A clause that opens with a question word after a verb of telling or asking must be tied to that verb's clause; any relation is enough. Untied it is L6.\" And \"tell me where it is\" untied → L6.\n\nBy analogy, the note's content clause must be tied to \"note\"; untied → L6? But in 300 it is a conjunct, arguably \"tied\" to c1 via k1... a coordination is a tie of sorts but between c2 and c1, not to n5 (\"the note\").\n\nHmm, this is a large family (14 rows) and my grades will matter. Let me look at these more carefully, one by one, and decide whether the coordination reading is acceptable per the rules' leniency (\"Which earlier wire a coordination joins, when grammar allows several\" — not applicable).\n\nRule L3: \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct.\" Rule L4: \"a relation or a coordination between wires that the message does not relate.\"\n\nFor 300: the message relates \"schedule a meeting ... with the note\" and \"bring the Q3 figures\" as the note's content. A coordination of the two clauses is a coordination the message partially relates (they are in the same sentence, connected by \"with the note\" — hmm, actually the surface shows \"with the note bring the Q3 figures\": there's no explicit coordination word).\n\nHonest assessment: the circuit's coordination claims that \"bring the Q3 figures\" is a second thing commanded, side by side with \"schedule a meeting\". That's a wrong statement the next layer must undo. I'll reject 300 as L3 noting the content of 'the note' is made a separate command.\n\nHmm, hold on. Alternatively \"L6: A pairing the words give is not written\" fits better: the pairing \"note ↔ bring the Q3 figures\" is not written. But it IS written (k1) — as a coordination, which is a wrong tie.\n\nI'll go with L3 for these unless the sop ties the content to the note.\n\nNow let me check each of the 14:\n- 300 (row 126): content c2 imperative, k1 with c1 → reject L3.\n- 301 (row 127): \"notes\" = fragment c3; content c2 imperative; k1 (c1,c3,c2). Content not tied to n5. Also the coordination joins three wires where the message has a label + content. Reject L3? Hmm, this one: c3 fragment \"notes\" is a conjunct; content c2 a conjunct. The pairing \"notes → discuss the vendor contract\" not written. Also a coordination with 3 conjuncts where the second is a fragment... I'd reject L3.\n- 302 (row 128): \"create a Hiring sync on Thursday at 11 and add the note Review the three shortlisted CVs\": sop has c1 create; k1 and c1 c2; c2 imperative add complement n4(the note); c3 nonfinite verb=\"Review\" complement n5(the three shortlisted CVs); r3 from n4 to c3 ✓ — content tied to the note as a nonfinite clause. Good! Is \"Review\" as nonfinite verb OK? \"Review the three shortlisted CVs\" is an imperative, but as content of a note it's a nonfinite-ish clause. Hmm, \"Review\" written as nonfinite verb with complement — plausible (content of note). The pairing is written. L8? The content's form: \"Review the three shortlisted CVs\" is a command, written nonfinite. Per settled case: content may be \"a nominal, or a clause tied to the word of saying\" — form not specified. I'll accept L8.\n- 303 (row 129): \"can you set up a meeting with Priya on Friday at 3 and write in the notes that we'll go through the Q4 roadmap?\" — c2 form=interrogative verb=write modifier=in complement=the notes; r4 that from n6 to c3 (content tied ✓). c3 declarative 'll go through the Q4 roadmap ✓. But c2 is written interrogative: \"and write in the notes that...\" — after \"can you\", the second verb shares the auxiliary and subject (\"can you ... and write\") — per settled case: \"a verb after 'and' that follows a clause with a subject and an auxiliary, in the form that auxiliary takes, may share them: a clause with no subject of its own, declarative or nonfinite. Written as a command, imperative, it passes too.\" Here c2 is written interrogative — is that allowed? The case allows declarative or nonfinite (or imperative). Interrogative isn't listed. Hmm. \"can you set up ... and write in the notes ...?\" — the whole is one question; \"write\" shares \"can you\". Writing the second conjunct as interrogative (with no subject/aux of its own) — it's not clearly wrong; it says the clause is a question, which is true of the whole question. Hmm, the case says declarative or nonfinite passes, imperative passes; interrogative not mentioned. \n\nI'd accept: the form leniency for a shared-subject coordination conjunct. Hmm, but the case explicitly enumerates the acceptable forms for the shared case: \"a clause with no subject of its own, declarative or nonfinite. Written as a command, imperative, it passes too.\" Enumerating declarative/nonfinite/imperative and not interrogative suggests interrogative fails. But that settled case is about a statement with \"and\" (\"the Gate box should go right under Path, and align their tops\"), not a question. For a question, \"can you X and Y?\" — the second clause interrogative is natural.\n\nI'll accept L8 for 303.\n\n- 304 (row 130): \"please add a meeting with Mihai next Tuesday at 9:30 with the agenda Budget overrun and next steps\"\nsop: c1 add complement n1; r1 with n1→n2; r2 at from c1 to n4; r3 with from n1 to n5(the agenda); n6 \"Budget overrun\"; k1 and conjunct n6 n7(next steps); r4 n1→n3; r5 n5→k1.\nGood: agenda content = coordination of two nominals, tied to \"agenda\". L8.\n\n- 305 (row 131): \"put a Supplier call on Dec 3 at 4pm, note: ask about delivery dates\"\nsop: c1 put; c2 imperative note; c3 imperative ask; r3 about c3→n5; k1 (c1,c2,c3); r4 n2→n3.\nFaults: \"note\" as an imperative verb — the message's \"note:\" is a label introducing the note's text. Making \"note\" a verb command is a wrong statement (L1: verb is a word that is no verb here? \"note\" IS a verb, but not here — here it's a label). Also \"ask about delivery dates\" is the content of the note, not a command side by side.\nGrade: L1 (verb wrong: \"note\" is no verb here)? Or L3. Hmm. I'd say L1: the circuit writes a clause of \"note\" that the message doesn't have (a clause the message doesn't have → L1 \"A clause that the circuit writes is wrong\"). Yes: L1.\n\nCorrection:\n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"put\" complement=$n1\n@n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"a\" dependent=\"Supplier\" head=\"call\"\n@r1 syntax.relation attachment=ambiguous marker=\"on\" from=$c1 to=$n2\n@n2 syntax.nominal head=\"Dec\"\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"3\"\n@r2 syntax.relation marker=\"at\" from=$c1 to=$n4\n@n4 syntax.nominal head=\"4pm\"\n@n5 syntax.nominal head=\"note\"\n@c2 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"ask\"\n@r3 syntax.relation marker=\"about\" from=$c2 to=$n6\n@n6 syntax.nominal dependent=\"delivery\" head=\"dates\"\n@r4 syntax.relation from=$n2 to=$n3\n@r5 syntax.relation from=$n5 to=$c2\n\nHmm, but \"note\" not tied to c1? Add r6: relation from c1 to n5 (apposition)? The \"note: ask...\" is part of the message. Let me tie n5 to c1 with a relation: @r6 syntax.relation from=$c1 to=$n5. Good.\n\n- 306 (row 132): \"I'd like a meeting with Lee and Ana on Wednesday at noon; in the notes put Bring laptops for the demo\"\nsop: c1 'd like complement n2; r1 with n2→k1; r2 on n2→n5; r3 at from c1 to n6(noon); r4 in from c2 to n7(the notes); c2 nonfinite verb=put; c3 imperative Bring complement laptops; r5 for c3→n9; k2 coordination c1 c3; r6 c3→c2.\nFaults: c2 \"put\" written nonfinite, c3 \"Bring laptops for the demo\" imperative, r6 ties c3 to c2 (\"Bring...\" tied to \"put\"). The content of the notes: \"put Bring laptops for the demo in the notes\" — here \"put\" is the command verb with \"Bring laptops for the demo\" as the content of the notes being put in. Hmm: the actual sentence: \"in the notes put Bring laptops for the demo\" = \"put 'Bring laptops for the demo' in the notes\". So \"put\" is the main verb of that clause (imperative) and \"Bring laptops for the demo\" is the content.\nIn sop: c2 nonfinite verb=put — \"put\" is the command verb, written nonfinite → L1? Its form: the clause \"in the notes put Bring laptops for the demo\" is a command → imperative. Written nonfinite with r6 tying c3→c2. Hmm. Also \"Bring\" is written as an imperative command (the content) — form-wise, content of a note as a command is plausible.\nThe bigger issue: the command \"put\" is written nonfinite, not imperative. Per L1: form wrong for a command? L1 says \"a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\". Command as nonfinite isn't listed. Hmm. But there's a subtler issue: is \"put\" the verb of a clause whose form should be imperative — yes, \"put\" is the command. The circuit's clause c2 nonfinite with verb put, tied to c3 by r6 and holding \"in the notes\" — the next layer would read \"put\" as a nonfinite verb, i.e. not a command, and \"Bring laptops\" as a separate command coordinated with c1 (k2 c1 c3). So the message's request \"put X in the notes\" is lost — it becomes \"Bring laptops for the demo\" as a command alongside \"I'd like a meeting\".\n\nWait: k2 coordination joins c1 (I'd like a meeting) with c3 (imperative Bring laptops...). And c2 (put ... in the notes) is tied to c3 by r6. So the reading: \"I'd like a meeting ... ; bring laptops for the demo (put in the notes)\". Hmm, the content-tie is there via r6 (c3 tied to c2 = put). Actually that's not bad: \"Bring laptops for the demo\" is the thing put in the notes. The real fault: \"put\" as nonfinite (form) and the coordination k2 joining c1 with c3 (a command) — that's a coordination of an imperative with a declarative: allowed (\"so please add a calendar\" pattern: c1 declarative + c3 imperative coordination — fine).\n\nHmm, is \"put\" nonfinite an L1 form error? \"put\" here is the finite imperative verb of \"in the notes put Bring laptops\". Written nonfinite, its command status is lost — the next layer sees c2 as a nonfinite verb \"put\" (like an infinitive), yet c2 has no subject and is the head of the \"in the notes\" phrase. The message's command \"put ... in the notes\" is thereby not written as a command. I think L1 (form of a clause with the message's command verb wrong) — but the L1 list doesn't include command→nonfinite. Hmm, but it does say \"A clause that the circuit writes is wrong. Its form is wrong: ...\" with examples.\n\nGiven \"can the next layer continue?\" — reading c2 nonfinite \"put\" with c3 tied to it, the next layer can reconstruct \"put Bring laptops for the demo in the notes\". I lean L8 for 306? Hmm, but the k2 coordination joining c1 and c3 (skipping c2, which is between them in the sentence and belongs to c3) — \"A coordination with a clause between two of its conjuncts that belongs to none of them fails: L3.\" c2 is between c1 and c3 (in the sentence c2 comes before c3) and belongs to... c3 (r6 c3→c2), not to c1. So the coordination k2 (c1, c3) has c2 between them belonging to none of the conjuncts (well, to c3 via r6). The rule says \"belongs to none of them fails: L3\". c2 belongs to c3 (a relation c3→c2 exists), so it's not \"none of them\"? Hmm, ambiguous — it belongs to c3. So it passes? Ugh.\n\nThis is getting long. Let me make decisions and move on with reasonable calls:\n\nRow 126 (300): reject L3 (note content made a separate command).\nRow 127 (301): reject L3 (same, plus the \"notes\" fragment as conjunct).\nRow 128 (302): L8.\nRow 129 (303): L8.\nRow 130 (304): L8.\nRow 131 (305): reject L1 (\"note\" made a verb of an imperative clause; \"ask about delivery dates\" is its content).\nRow 132 (306): L8? Let me reconsider... The message: \"I'd like a meeting with Lee and Ana on Wednesday at noon; in the notes put Bring laptops for the demo\". Circuit: c2 nonfinite \"put\" holds \"in the notes\"; c3 imperative \"Bring laptops for the demo\"; r5 for c3→the demo; r6 c3→c2; k2 c1+c3.\nReading: notes ← (put...) ← Bring laptops. Reasonably close. The imperative form of \"put\" lost, but c3 is imperative and c2 nonfinite. I'll accept L8 (plausible, next layer can continue; nothing explicit lost except form nuance).\n\nHmm, but wait: is anything lost? \"in the notes put X\" — the word \"put\" is there, \"in the notes\" there, X tied. Fine. L8.\n\nRow 133 (307): \"schedule Onboarding for the 24th at 10am and attach the note Dial-in code 4471\"\nsop: c1 schedule complement Onboarding; r1 for c1→n2; r2 at c1→n3; k1 and c1 c2; c2 imperative attach complement n4(the note); n5 \"Dial-in code\"; n6 \"4471\"; r3 n4→n5; r4 n5→n6. Good: content \"Dial-in code 4471\" tied to the note as nominals. L8.\n\nRow 134 (308): \"Priya always forgets the slides, so when you book our Monday 11am meeting, add a note saying Priya brings the slides\"\nsop: c1 forgets; r1 marker=\"so when\" from c1 to c2; c2 declarative book subject you complement n4(our meeting); n5 Monday; n6 11am; c3 imperative add complement n7(a note); c4 nonfinite saying; n8 Priya; c5 declarative brings subject Priya complement the slides; k1 coordination c1 c3; c6 fragment complement n5; r2 n5→n6; r3 n7→c4; r4 c4→c5.\nSame \"so when\" merged-marker issue as n=163 and the \"when\" clause starting at c1: \"when you book our Monday 11am meeting\" belongs with \"add a note\". Started at c1 (Priya forgets) → wrong start (L3), like n=163.\nAlso n5 \"Monday\" is in a fragment c6 (not in the meeting nominal?): n4 = \"our meeting\", n5 Monday, n6 11am, c6 fragment complement n5, r2 n5→n6. So \"Monday 11am\" floats as a fragment tied... c6 is a conjunct of k1 (c1, c3) — no wait, k1 conjunct=c1 and c3 only; c6 is not in k1, and nothing relates c6 except complement of c6 holds n5. So c6 is untied → L6. Also \"Monday 11am\" should belong with \"our ... meeting\".\nSo multiple faults: L3 (the \"when\" clause start) — first applicable. I'll grade L3 with note about the 'so when' start, and mention Monday 11am lost. Note must be one sentence; I can mention both.\n\nCorrection:\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"Priya\"\n@c1 syntax.clause form=declarative modifier=\"always\" verb=\"forgets\" subject=$n1 complement=$n2\n@n2 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" head=\"slides\"\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"you\"\n@n4 syntax.nominal dependent=\"our\" head=\"meeting\" dependent=\"Monday\" dependent=\"11am\"\n@c2 syntax.clause form=declarative verb=\"book\" subject=$n3 complement=$n4\n@c3 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"add\" complement=$n5\n@n5 syntax.nominal dependent=\"a\" head=\"note\"\n@c4 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"saying\"\n@r1 syntax.relation from=$n5 to=$c4\n@n6 syntax.nominal head=\"Priya\"\n@c5 syntax.clause form=declarative verb=\"brings\" subject=$n6 complement=$n7\n@n7 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" head=\"slides\"\n@r2 syntax.relation from=$c4 to=$c5\n@r3 syntax.relation marker=\"when\" from=$c3 to=$c2\n@r4 syntax.relation marker=\"so\" from=$c1 to=$c3\n\nGood: all words covered? Priya always forgets the slides so when you book our Monday 11am meeting add a note saying Priya brings the slides. ✓\n\nRow 135 (309): \"ok so set up a call with Mihai on Thursday at 4 - and could you jot down in the notes: confirm the hotel booking\"\nsop: c1 imperative ok so set up complement n1; r1 with n1→n2; r2 on n1→n3; r3 at n3→n4; k1 and: c1, c2, c3; c2 interrogative could jot down subject you; r4 in c2→n6; c3 imperative confirm complement n7.\nIssues: k1 joins c1 (imperative), c2 (interrogative) and c3 (imperative). The settled case: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3\" — that's about a clause standing inside another via a marker relation. Here no marker relation: c1, c2, c3 joined by \"and\". A coordination of an imperative with an interrogative: \"set up a call ... and could you jot down ...\". Both are requests; joining a command and a question — the message does relate them (\"and\"). Fine. And c3 \"confirm the hotel booking\" is the content of the notes → not tied to \"the notes\" (n6); it's a conjunct of k1. Wrong conjunct / content not tied → reject L3? Hmm.\n\nHmm: \"jot down in the notes: confirm the hotel booking\" — content. c3 imperative coordinated — same fault as 300. But here c3 is a conjunct after c2 which holds \"in the notes\". Hmm.\n\nI'd reject: L3 (the coordination has a wrong conjunct: 'confirm the hotel booking' is the content of the notes, not a third command). Or L6. I'll use L3 for consistency with 300/301.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider 300/301/309 to be fair. Is \"content of notes as an imperative clause tied to nothing\" a wrong statement or a lost pairing? The message's information: the note's text. If the circuit has the note (n5) and a separate imperative clause, the next layer must decide that the imperative is the note's content — that's an undone inference? The circuit says these are two commands. I think rejecting is right: it states a command the message doesn't give (you're not asked to bring figures; you're asked to note them).\n\nOK. So 300 L3, 301 L3, 309 L3.\n\nRow 136 (310): \"I'm meeting Lee on Friday at 10 to talk pricing. Please put that in, with the note that he wants the competitor analysis first\"\nsop: c1 'm meeting; r1 on c1→n3; r2 at c1→n4; r3 to c1→c2; c2 nonfinite talk complement pricing; c3 imperative Please put modifier=in complement n6(that); r4 with c3→n7(the note); r5 that n7→c4; c4 declarative wants subject he complement n9; \"first\" as modifier of c4. Good. L8.\n\nRow 137 (311): \"book a Design sync with Ana tomorrow at 3 with notes Share Figma link, and cancel the yoga\"\nsop: c1 imperative book modifier=tomorrow complement n1; r1 with n1→n2; r2 at c1→n3; r3 with c1→n4(notes); c2 nonfinite verb=Share complement n5(Figma link); k1 and conjunct=c2 c3; c3 nonfinite verb=cancel complement n6(the yoga); r4 from n4 to k1.\nIssues: the coordination k1 joins c2 and c3: \"Share Figma link\" (note content) and \"cancel the yoga\" (a command). The message: \"... with notes Share Figma link, and cancel the yoga\" — \"and\" joins \"book ...\" (c1) with \"cancel the yoga\". The circuit joins c2 (content) with c3 → wrong conjunct? Per the settled case: \"The earlier wire a coordination joins is the wire that ends right before the conjunction, or a wire that holds it.\" Right before \"and\" stands \"Share Figma link\" (c2) → joining c2 and c3 is allowed by that rule! But then \"cancel the yoga\" reads as coordinated with the note content, and \"book\" is not joined with \"cancel\".\nHmm, and c1 (book) is tied to n4 (notes) → r4 n4→k1: the notes hold the coordination. So reading: \"book a Design sync ... with notes [Share Figma link and cancel the yoga]\" — that makes \"cancel the yoga\" the note's content too. That's a wrong statement: the message commands cancel the yoga.\nAlso both c2 and c3 are nonfinite: \"cancel the yoga\" is a command written nonfinite (form: it shares... no subject/aux before; \"book\" is imperative with no subject). Per the pattern: \"a verb after 'and' that follows a clause with a subject and an auxiliary...\" — not applicable (no aux). So \"cancel the yoga\" should be imperative. Written nonfinite → form wrong? Per L1 first item examples, command as nonfinite isn't listed. Hmm.\n\nThe clearer fault: \"cancel the yoga\" placed as part of the notes' content (via r4 n4→k1) rather than a command joined to \"book\". That's a wrong statement → L3 (wrong conjunct: the coordination k1 should join c1 with the cancel clause).\n\nGrade L3. Correction:\n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"book\" modifier=\"tomorrow\" complement=$n1\n@n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"a\" dependent=\"Design\" head=\"sync\"\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"with\" from=$n1 to=$n2\n@n2 syntax.nominal head=\"Ana\"\n@r2 syntax.relation attachment=ambiguous marker=\"at\" from=$c1 to=$n3\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"3\"\n@r3 syntax.relation attachment=ambiguous marker=\"with\" from=$c1 to=$n4\n@n4 syntax.nominal head=\"notes\"\n@c2 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"Share\" complement=$n5\n@n5 syntax.nominal dependent=\"Figma\" head=\"link\"\n@r4 syntax.relation from=$n4 to=$c2\n@c3 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"cancel\" complement=$n6\n@n6 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" head=\"yoga\"\n@k1 syntax.coordination conjunction=\"and\" conjunct=$c1 conjunct=$c3\n\nRow 138 (312): \"if there's still time on Wednesday at 2, add a meeting with Priya and note that it's about the parental leave handover\"\nsop: r1 if from c2 to c1; c1 declarative 's subject there complement time; r2 on n2→n3; r3 at n3→n4; c2 imperative add complement n5; r4 with n5→n6; k1 and c2 c3; c3 imperative note; r5 that c3→c4; c4 declarative 's subject it; r6 about c4→n8.\nGood: \"note that it's about...\" — c3 imperative note with content clause c4 tied ✓. L8.\n\nRow 139 (313): \"would you create a Board prep meeting next Monday at 8:30? Notes should say: print 10 copies of the deck\"\nsop: c1 interrogative would create subject you complement n2; n3 next Monday; r1 at from n2 to n4; n5 Notes; c2 declarative should say subject n5; c3 imperative print complement n6(10 copies); r2 of n6→n7; r3 n2→n3; k1 coordination c2 c3.\nFault: \"print 10 copies of the deck\" is the content of what Notes should say; written as an imperative clause joined in coordination with c2 → same fault as 300. Should be tied to c2 (or to \"say\"). k1 joins c2 and c3 though — hmm, a coordination is a tie between them... \"Notes should say: print 10 copies of the deck\" — the content clause should be tied to \"say\". Coordinating the content with the saying clause is wrong-ish (it claims two statements). Reject L3? \n\nHmm, how consistent: for 139, the content is coordinated with its saying clause (not with a distant command), so the pairing \"say ↔ content\" is at least present as a coordination. A next layer would read \"Notes should say\" + coordination with the imperative print. It can infer. Hmm.\n\nCompare 300: content coordinated with the *main command* (schedule), where the notes-nominal is a separate complement. Also 309: content as third conjunct.\n\nLet me think about what's fair: the rule for content says it must be \"a clause tied to the word of saying\". Is a coordination \"a tie\"? The schema calls coordination \"wires put side by side\". A relation is a tie. Hmm, the L6 rule says \"A clause or a nominal is tied to nothing inside its sentence\" — coordination probably counts as being tied (it's in a wire with others). \n\nI'll adopt: content coordinated with the clause holding the word of saying (139: k1 c2+c3) → acceptable (the pairing is written). Content coordinated with a different clause, away from the note nominal (300, 301, 309) → the pairing is not written → reject L3.\n\nHmm, but for 300, is the content \"coordinated with a different clause\"? c2 is coordinated with c1 which holds the note as complement (r4 with c1→n5). So the note is in c1's complement and the content is c2 coordinated with c1. So the pairing note↔content is also \"close\". Ugh!\n\nThen 300 would be acceptable too... By that logic, 139 acceptable, 300 acceptable, 301 acceptable (c3 fragment \"notes\" is a conjunct holding the notes!), 309 acceptable (c2 holds \"in the notes\", c3 coordinated).\n\nHmm! That's a consistent lenient policy: content as a coordinated clause in the same sentence passes.\n\nWhich policy to adopt? The leniency instruction (\"a plausible or incomplete reading that the next layer can continue from is acceptable\") favors acceptance. And the settled L2 case about content concerns words held as `discourse` (a clear loss). Here the content is not in discourse; it's a clause coordinated/tied within the sentence.\n\nLet me adopt the lenient policy: content of a note/agenda held as a clause coordinated with the clause that mentions the note, or as a nominal — acceptable (L8). Only when the content is held as `discourse` (L2) or lost in a nominal head (L3) is it a fault.\n\nUnder that policy:\n- 300 L8\n- 301: c3 \"notes\" fragment is a conjunct, content c2 is a conjunct → L8? The \"notes\" fragment as a conjunct of the coordination is odd: three conjuncts (c1, c3-fragment \"notes\", c2-imperative). Hmm, that says \"notes\" is put side by side as a unit. Is that a wrong statement? It's a label + content. A next layer can read it. I lean L8 under leniency... but the fragment conjunct is strange. Hmm.\n\nLet me instead judge 301 by its own merits: \"book a call with Lee tomorrow at 2pm, notes: discuss the vendor contract\". Circuit: n1 call, n5 \"notes\" (in c3 fragment), c2 discuss complement. Reading: book a call..., notes; discuss the vendor contract. It's recoverable. L8.\n\n- 305: \"note\" written as an imperative verb: \"put a Supplier call on Dec 3 at 4pm, note: ask about delivery dates\" → c2 imperative verb=\"note\" (no complement), c3 imperative ask. Is \"note\" a verb here? The message says \"note:\" as a label; reading it as a command \"note: ask about delivery dates\" is a plausible reading (as if telling the assistant to note the following). Hmm! Actually that reading is quite plausible: \"note: ask about delivery dates\" = \"note the following\". With leniency, L8? The circuit writes a clause of \"note\" whose form is imperative with no complement — a command \"note\". Then \"ask about delivery dates\" as another command. A next layer can continue: it'd think \"note ... and ask about delivery dates\" — hmm, it loses that both are note text.\n\nUnder my lenient policy, this passes as the same structure (label content as a coordinated command)? The difference: here \"note\" itself is made a verb. The words \"note:\" — treating it as a verb is \"a word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb\" (the rules explicitly allow such readings: \"A word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb\" is where several readings pass). \"note\" is such a word. So L8.\n\nHmm, OK. And 306 (put nonfinite)? L8 under leniency. And 307 L8, 310 L8, 311 — the \"cancel the yoga\" as part of the notes' content (r4 n4→k1). Is that a wrong statement? Under leniency, the coordination joins c2 (last wire before \"and\") with c3 per the rule \"The earlier wire a coordination joins is the wire that ends right before the conjunction\". And the notes tie to k1. So \"cancel the yoga\" ends up as note content — a wrong statement? The next layer reads: book a Design sync ... with notes [Share Figma link and cancel the yoga] → it would think the note says both. Hmm, that IS a wrong statement about the message: it makes \"cancel the yoga\" a note's content rather than a command to do. I'll reject 311 L3.\n\nFor 300/301/309/139: content as a conjunct of the coordination — the coordination joins content with the saying clause. That is not \"note content + another command\" inversion; the content is at least next to the note words. Accept.\n\nFor 309: k1 joins c1, c2, c3 — c3 \"confirm the hotel booking\" is coordinated with c2 which holds \"in the notes\". Accept under leniency.\n\nFor 305: accept.\n\nHmm, but 300: c2 \"bring the Q3 figures\" coordinated with c1 which holds \"with the note\". Accept.\n\nOK so the note-family rows I accept: 300, 301, 305, 306, 307, 309, 310, 313, 302, 303, 304. And reject 308 (L3 wrong start of \"when\" relation, like 163) and 311 (L3).\n\nHmm, wait. For 308 the rejection is about \"so when\" merged and the when-clause starting at \"Priya always forgets\" + the untied \"Monday 11am\" fragment. I'm comfortable rejecting (it also loses \"Monday 11am\" from the meeting).\n\nNow, let me reconsider 309: does it have \"notes\" tied? r4 in c2→n6 ✓. Fine.\n\nLet me now also handle remaining rows 140-149. I haven't read them yet. Let me read them next.\n\nFor now, records for rows 124-139:\n124 (298) L8\n125 (299) L8\n126 (300) L8\n127 (301) L8\n128 (302) L8\n129 (303) L8\n130 (304) L8\n131 (305) L8\n132 (306) L8\n133 (307) L8\n134 (308) L3\n135 (309) L8\n136 (310) L8\n137 (311) L3\n138 (312) L8\n139 (313) L8\n\nHmm, that's a big shift to leniency for this family. Let me double check 313 and 302 once more for other defects:\n302: c3 nonfinite verb=\"Review\" complement=\"the three shortlisted CVs\", tied to n4 (the note) ✓. Good.\n313: c2 \"Notes should say\" — subject n5 Notes ✓, aux should, verb say ✓; content c3 imperative coordinated ✓ (lenient). r1 at from n2 to n4: \"at 8:30\" starting at the meeting nominal ✓. r3 n2→n3 (\"next Monday\" tied to meeting) ✓. Good.\n\nAlso check 306 again: k2 joins c1 and c3 where c2 is between them; c2 belongs to c3 (r6). Under the settled rule, this passes (c2 belongs to a conjunct). ✓ L8.\n\nNow read the last rows 140-149.",
