    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "Judge rows 140-149.\n\nRow 140 (n=314): \"meeting with Ana, note: bring the contract\"\nsop: c1 nonfinite verb=\"meeting\"; r1 with c1→n1(Ana); c2 imperative verb=\"note\"; c3 imperative bring complement n2(the contract); r2 from c2 to c1; k1 c2 c3.\nFault: \"meeting\" as verb of a nonfinite clause — noun made verb → L1 (as n=278/291/297). Though here \"meeting\" could be a participle?? \"meeting with Ana, note: bring the contract\" — \"meeting\" is a noun (a meeting event). So L1.\n\nCorrection:\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"meeting\"\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"with\" from=$n1 to=$n2\n@n2 syntax.nominal head=\"Ana\"\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"note\"\n@c1 syntax.clause form=fragment complement=$n1\n@r2 syntax.relation from=$c1 to=$n3\n@c2 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"bring\" complement=$n4\n@n4 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" head=\"contract\"\n@r3 syntax.relation from=$n3 to=$c2\n\nHmm, \"note: bring the contract\" — content. Keep \"note\" as a nominal tied to the fragment clause, and the content clause tied to \"note\" as nonfinite. Good. But should the content be a clause with verb \"bring\" (nonfinite) — yes.\n\nRow 141 (n=315): \"call with Lee tomorrow - agenda: pricing\"\nsop: c1 form=imperative verb=\"call\" modifier=tomorrow; r1 with c1→n1(Lee); n2 agenda; n3 pricing; r2 from c2 to c1; c2 fragment complement n2; r3 n2→n3.\nFault: \"call\" as imperative verb — \"call with Lee tomorrow\" is not a command; it's a noun phrase (\"call\" = meeting). Hmm! \"call\" can be a verb, but here \"call with Lee\" is a noun. Per the settled case: \"A command needs its verb. When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb ('book design review', 'schedule the kickoff'), it is the verb; held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1.\" The reverse: is holding \"call\" as a verb wrong here? The message \"call with Lee tomorrow - agenda: pricing\" — no other verb. If \"call\" is a verb, the message is a command \"call with Lee tomorrow\" — but \"call with Lee\" as a command is ungrammatical (\"call with\" is not a verb+preposition phrase for phoning Lee... actually \"call with Lee\" could mean \"call [someone] with Lee\"?). Hmm, we can't use world knowledge. \n\nThe trap in the settled case is one-directional: a command must not lose its verb. Here the circuit reads the message as a command. Is that a wrong statement? \"call with Lee tomorrow\" as a command — a reader could go \"call [me] with Lee tomorrow\"? Reads oddly. But grammar: \"call\" as a verb, \"with Lee\" as a relation, \"tomorrow\" modifier. Hmm, plausible enough? The message has \"agenda: pricing\" as a separate label. Since \"call\" can be a verb and the words don't settle it (\"A word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb\" — several readings pass), I'd accept L8 under that rule.\n\nHmm, but the settled case says when the first word can be a noun or verb, it must be the verb (else L1). That's when it's a command. Here the reading \"call\" = verb gives a command; the reading \"call\" = noun gives a fragment. The formalizer chose verb → allowed by the \"word that grammar allows as noun or verb\" leniency. Accept L8.\n\nRow 142 (n=317): \"Retro Friday, notes: what went wrong with the release\"\nsop: n1 Retro; n2 Friday; n3 notes; n4 what; c1 form=interrogative verb=went modifier=wrong subject=n4(what); r1 with c1→n5(the release); c2 fragment complement n1, n2, n3; k1 conjunct c2 c1.\nFault: \"what went wrong with the release\" — a clause opening with a question word inside another clause (the content of the notes) → per settled case, its form is declarative; written interrogative = L1. Also \"what\" as the subject — hmm, \"what went wrong\": \"what\" is the subject of \"went\" ✓ (that's how grammar puts it: \"the head of a nominal in 'what is this?'\" — here n4 head \"what\" with subject role ✓).\nSo L1 for the interrogative. Quote: 'what went wrong with the release'.\n\nCorrection:\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"Retro\"\n@n2 syntax.nominal head=\"Friday\"\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"notes\"\n@n4 syntax.nominal head=\"what\"\n@c1 syntax.clause form=declarative verb=\"went\" modifier=\"wrong\" subject=$n4\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"with\" from=$c1 to=$n5\n@n5 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" head=\"release\"\n@c2 syntax.clause form=fragment complement=$n1 complement=$n2 complement=$n3\n@k1 syntax.coordination conjunct=$c2 conjunct=$c1\n\nGood (content clause tied by coordination; lenient policy).\n\nRow 143 (n=318): \"lunch with Lee Thursday, remember the gift card\" — c1 imperative remember complement n4; fragment c2 lunch; k1; r2 n1→n3(Thursday). Good. L8.\n\nRow 144 (n=319): \"new meeting, notes say parking is on level 2\"\nsop: n1 new meeting; n2 notes; c1 declarative verb=say subject=n2; n3 parking; c2 declarative verb=is subject=parking; r1 on c2→n4(level); n5 \"2\"; c3 fragment complement n1; k1 c3 c1; r2 c1→c2; r3 n4→n5.\nGood. L8.\n\nRow 145 (n=322): \"can you book a meeting with Lee, Priya and Mihai at a time they're all available?\"\nsop: c1 interrogative can book subject you complement n2; r1 with n2→k1 (3 names); r2 at n2→n6(a time); n7 they; c2 declarative verb='re modifier=available subject=they complement all; r3 n6→c2. \n\"they're all available\" — \"all\" as complement nominal; fine. L8.\n\nRow 146 (n=323): \"put a 30 minute check-in with Ana at the earliest opening on Thursday\" — L8.\n\nRow 147 (n=325): \"Lee and Ana are hard to pin down, so just pick a time that works for both of them and set up the meeting\"\nsop: n1 Lee; k1 and n1 n2; c1 declarative are modifier=hard subject=k1; r1 to c1→c2(nonfinite pin, modifier down); k2 coordination conjunction=\"so\" conjunction=\"and\" conjunct=c1, c3, c5; c3 imperative just pick complement n3(a time); r2 that n3→c4(works); r3 for c4→n4(both); r4 of n4→n5(them); c5 imperative set up complement n6(the meeting).\nIssue: k2 has two conjunctions (\"so\" and \"and\") for one coordination joining three conjuncts (c1, c3, c5). The message: \"…, so just pick a time … and set up the meeting\". \"so\" joins c1 with c3; \"and\" joins c3 with c5. Writing both as conjunctions of one coordination — is that a wrong statement? Hmm. Compare row 57 (n=57) where I rejected a coordination with both \"so\" and \"and\" because \"so\" at the start joined nothing. Here both conjunctions do join real things, but they join different pairs. A single coordination with two conjunctions is redundant yet conveys c1+c3+c5 related by so/and. The next layer can continue: it reads three coordinated clauses with \"so\"/\"and\". I'd accept L8 — hmm.\n\nHmm, but strictly: a coordination's conjunction is one; two different conjunctions joining different pairs is a wrong statement (the \"and\" pair vs \"so\" pair). Under leniency: the three clauses are indeed joined in the message in sequence, with \"so\" between the first two and \"and\" between the last two. A single coordination holding both conjunctions roughly represents \"c1 so c3 and c5\". I'll accept L8, consistent with my leniency (it doesn't have to undo a wrong claim about which clauses are joined).\n\nFor row 57 (n=57) I rejected because \"so\" joined nothing (no earlier clause) — a coordination claiming \"so\" links two commands when \"so\" is an opener. Fine, keep.\n\nRow 148 (n=326): \"the server went down last night. Get me a meeting with Mihai at the soonest free moment, please\" — good. L8.\n\nRow 149 (n=327): \"if Friday is open at all, grab the first free half hour for a chat with Priya\"\nsop: r1 if from c2 to c1; n1 Friday; c1 declarative verb=is modifier=open modifier=at modifier=all subject=Friday; c2 imperative grab complement n2(the first free half hour); r2 for c2→n3(a chat); r3 with n3→n4(Priya).\n\"at all\" as modifiers ✓. L8.\n\nNow tally for the whole packet — rejections:\nn=22 L3, n=39 L1, n=47 L3, n=57 L3, n=60 L1, n=62 L1, n=72 L3, n=99 L1, n=109 L3, n=113 L3, n=150 L1, n=163 L3, n=186 L3, n=202 L3, n=260 L1, n=278 L1, n=285 L1, n=291 