    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "My coverage checker is crude: it treats \"form=declarative\" values as words (my regex captured form values too since I matched `=...`). Let me refine by only taking keys in (head, dependent, verb, auxiliary, negation, subject, complement, modifier, discourse, marker, conjunct, conjunction, ellipsis, form...). Actually exclude form/attachment keys.\n\nKey real findings:\n- n=22: missing \"let's\" — because text token is \"let's\" while my sop has \"let\" (verb) + \"'s\" (head). Tokenization splits it; that's consistent with the CSV patterns (e.g., row 22's original sop had head=\"'s\"). Fine.\n- n=113, 163: \"it's\" → split into \"it\" + \"'s\" ✓ fine.\n- n=291: missing \"meeting\" — my correction dropped \"meeting\"! Text: \"meeting with Mihai, Cedar room\". My sop: n1 Mihai, n2 Cedar room, c1 fragment complement n2, r1 with c1→n1. \"meeting\" is missing! Must fix.\n- n=314: \"note:\" vs \"note\" — tokenization of colon; but let me check my sop has \"note\" ✓ (n3 head=\"note\"). Fine.\n- n=317: \"notes:\" → n3 head=\"notes\" ✓ fine.\n\nSo only n=291 needs a fix. Let me correct: add n3 nominal head=\"meeting\" and make the fragment's complement = n3.\n\nNew sop for 291:\n@n1 syntax.nominal head=\"Mihai\"\n@n2 syntax.nominal dependent=\"Cedar\" head=\"room\"\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"meeting\"\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"with\" from=$n3 to=$n1\n@r2 syntax.relation from=$n3 to=$n2\n@c1 syntax.clause form=fragment complement=$n3\n\nGood.\n\nAlso, I should double check the coverage for every L8 note... the L8 rows have no sop so no coverage check needed.\n\nNow also verify a few of my rejections' sops more carefully for word coverage with a fixed checker. Let me fix the checker to use only content keys and re-run, including the fixed 291.\n\nLet me apply the fix via a targeted JSON rewrite (batch, rewriting the file — acceptable as a batch operation? It's editing one line... The instruction says never edit line by line; a single programmatic rebuild of the whole file is fine).\n\nLet me write a python that reads the file, fixes n=291's sop, and rewrites the whole file.",
